window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-649ffe45719449f8"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"codex-security-cookbook\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"codex\">Codex 安全配置手册</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取文档的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote>  <p>部署 Codex 时，需要决定它可以访问哪些账户、文件和服务，以及可以执行哪些操作。本手册帮助企业管理员将这些决定落实为配置、测试配置效果，并判断还需要哪些额外保护措施。</p> <p>这些安全建议旨在帮助你为开发者提供实用的 Codex 访问能力，同时降低意外改动、敏感数据泄露以及安全防护措施本身被更改的风险。请根据工作流程和风险要求选择合适的控制措施，了解它们的限制，并在自己的环境中验证其实际效果。这些建议是对组织现有身份、终端、网络和服务防护措施的补充。</p> <p>你可以从一项具体任务开始，例如限制用户只能登录公司工作区；也可以从更广泛的目标开始，例如在保护生产环境的同时让开发者使用 Codex。</p> <p>选择最符合当前工作的起点：</p> <ul> <li> <p><strong>配置方案</strong>逐项介绍单个控制措施，并提供设置说明、验证检查、回滚指导和适用边界。</p> </li> <li> <p><strong>组合方案</strong>展示如何将多项控制措施与身份、源代码管理、部署及其他系统中的防护结合起来，以实现常见的安全目标。</p> </li> </ul> <p>这些示例可作为起点，再按组织的实际情况调整。使用每个配置示例时，也要阅读其配套说明，确认配置应放在哪里、适用于哪些客户端或环境，以及它能控制和不能控制的范围。</p> <p>大范围部署前，先在测试终端上试用配置。检查预期工作流程能否成功，并确认刻意限制的操作确实会被阻止。根据结果完善配置，并向 Codex 使用者说明这些控制措施的边界。</p> <p><a id=\"choose-your-starting-point\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">选择起点</h2> <p>使用页面大纲打开下方相应章节。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>如果你想要</th> <th>从这里开始</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>配置某一项具体控制措施</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=configuration-recipes\">配置方案</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>在保护生产环境的同时让开发者使用 Codex</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\">在保护生产环境的同时让开发者使用 Codex</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>降低内部代码被公开发布的风险</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\">降低内部代码被公开发布的风险</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>防止 Codex 更改自身的安全防护措施</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\">防止 Codex 更改自身的安全防护措施</a></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>组合方案会针对每个目标列出相关控制措施、外部防护和验证方法。</p> <p><a id=\"start-here\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">从这里开始</h2> <ol> <li> <p>盘点本次涉及的使用界面和环境：桌面应用、CLI、IDE 扩展、Codex Cloud、CI 运行器和远程主机。</p> </li> <li> <p>大范围部署前，先在测试终端上试运行配置。</p> </li> <li> <p>记录最低支持的 Codex 版本。权限配置文件允许列表要求 Codex 0.138.0 或更高版本。</p> </li> <li> <p>为每个受管配置包保留一份回滚副本。</p> </li> <li> <p>每次更改后，都测试一个允许的工作流程和一个应被阻止的工作流程。</p> </li> </ol> <p>受管要求是管理员强制执行的限制；受管默认值则是启动时采用的初始设置。对于受支持的身份验证、沙盒、审批、MCP、插件、钩子和网络限制，应使用受管要求。身份验证要求必须通过本地方式或 macOS MDM 下发；云端受管要求不会强制执行这些身份验证限制。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"where-configuration-belongs\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_3\">配置应放在哪里</h3> <p>在向试点组部署前，请先根据组织情况调整这些配置示例。TOML 代码块是策略片段：将所需设置合并到现有文件中，不要重复定义表，并替换示例中的名称、域名和路径。验证清单描述的是预期结果，请在自己的环境中确认。</p> <p>对于本地 Codex，管理员可以在 macOS 和 Linux 上通过 <code>/etc/codex/requirements.toml</code> 部署强制要求；在 Windows 上则使用 <code>%ProgramData%\\OpenAI\\Codex\\requirements.toml</code>。在受管 macOS 设备上，MDM 也可以下发 <code>com.openai.codex:requirements_toml_base64</code> 偏好设置。还可以在管理控制台中打开 <strong>Agent Security</strong>，选择策略及其适用环境或操作系统，然后配置 <strong>Requirements</strong>，以设置云端受管策略。请保护策略文件及其父目录，避免 Codex 用户修改。普通 <code>config.toml</code> 或受管 Defaults 中的设置只是启动默认值，并非强制要求。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>和 <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"configuration-recipes\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">配置方案</h2> <p>选择需要的控制措施。打开相应方案，查看配置、验证清单、回滚指导和适用边界。验证后勾选相应项目；如果浏览器存储可用，进度会保存在此浏览器中。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>控制领域</th> <th>配置方案</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>身份验证</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\">限制为公司工作区登录</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>运行时权限</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\">限制权限配置文件和完全访问权限</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>MCP 服务器</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\">仅允许已批准的 MCP 服务器</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>监控与审计</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=monitor-codex-activity\">监控 Codex 活动</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>网络</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=restrict-network-destinations\">限制网络目标</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>文件和凭据</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\">保护机密信息及无关代码库</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>敏感命令</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\">要求敏感命令经过审批</a></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_5\">限制为公司工作区登录</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果你希望员工只能通过公司的 ChatGPT 工作区登录 Codex，并在受管终端上禁止使用个人 ChatGPT 账户和 API 密钥身份验证，请使用此方案。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>在公司管理的设备上，Codex 只接受通过 ChatGPT 登录到公司工作区。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"prerequisites\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_6\">准备条件</h4> <ul> <li> <p>已批准的 ChatGPT Enterprise 工作区 UUID。</p> </li> <li> <p>一种可向终端设备下发 Codex 受管配置的方式。</p> </li> <li> <p>已决定在受管终端上禁止使用 API 密钥身份验证。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"configure\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_7\">配置</h4> <ol> <li> <p>在内部配置代码库中记录已批准工作区的 UUID。</p> </li> <li> <p>在本地 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中添加强制身份验证要求：</p> </li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_login_methods = [\"chatgpt\"]\n   allowed_chatgpt_workspaces = [\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"]\n</code></pre> <p>第一个键只允许使用 ChatGPT 身份验证；第二个键将登录限制在已批准的工作区。请将示例 UUID 替换为你的工作区 UUID。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">requirements 参考</a>。</p> <ol> <li> <p>按照<a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=where-configuration-belongs\">配置应放在哪里</a>中的路径，通过本地 <code>requirements.toml</code> 或 macOS MDM requirements 部署。身份验证要求会在凭据加载和云端策略获取之前生效；Codex 不会通过云端受管 requirements 执行这些要求。请保护配置文件及其父目录，避免用户修改。旧版 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 默认设置不具备强制执行此限制的作用。</p> </li> <li> <p>试点期间，让用户退出登录，或移除先前保存的个人 Codex 会话。不要假设新策略会追溯性地使现有会话失效。</p> </li> <li> <p>将登录限制与工作区角色和群组访问控制结合使用。身份验证决定使用哪个身份登录；RBAC 决定该身份可以使用哪些 Codex 功能。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">身份验证</a>。</p> </li> </ol> <p><a id=\"verify\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_8\">验证</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 退出 Codex 登录。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试使用个人 ChatGPT 账户登录。预期结果：登录个人工作区会被拒绝。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试通过 API 密钥登录 Codex。预期结果：API 密钥登录会被拒绝。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 使用公司账户登录已批准的工作区。预期结果：登录成功。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 重启桌面应用、CLI 和 IDE 扩展，然后重复上述检查。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"roll-back\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_9\">回滚</h4> <p>恢复先前的本地身份验证要求，并重新部署经过审核的配置包。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_10\">适用边界</h4> <p>此方案控制的是受管终端上的受管 Codex 客户端。如果要求是“禁止在公司资产上使用个人 Codex”，还应配合终端应用控制和网络策略。</p>  <p><a id=\"restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">限制权限配置文件和完全访问权限</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果开发者只需要只读或限定在工作区内的访问权限，而你又希望禁止他们选择“完全访问”或通过 <code>--yolo</code> 绕过审批和沙盒，请使用此方案。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>用户只能选择已批准的只读或工作区范围权限配置文件，不能选择 <code>danger-full-access</code>、<code>approval_policy = \"never\"</code> 或使用 <code>--yolo</code>。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure_1\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_12\">配置</h4> <ol> <li>定义一个已批准的工作区权限配置文件：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [permissions.corp-workspace]\n   description = \"Company workspace access with no unrestricted host access.\"\n   extends = \":workspace\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>强制执行允许的权限配置文件和审批策略：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\", \"untrusted\"]\n   allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\n   default_permissions = \"corp-workspace\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \":read-only\" = true\n   \"corp-workspace\" = true\n   \":danger-full-access\" = false\n</code></pre> <p>该允许列表是完整列表：未列出或设为 false 的配置文件都会被拒绝，包括未来版本新增的配置文件。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。</p> <ol> <li>对于仍使用 <code>sandbox_mode</code> 的旧版客户端，请使用：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\", \"untrusted\"]\n   allowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>请将其作为管理员强制执行的要求下发，而不是放入用户可编辑的配置文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>如果之后要启用 Auto-review，请先测试，再有意地将其加入配置。Auto-review 改变的是由谁审核越界请求，不会恢复已经移除的边界。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">Auto-review</a>。</p> </li> </ol> <p><a id=\"verify_1\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_13\">验证</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 打开权限选择器。预期结果：“完全访问”选项不可用或会被拒绝。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 使用 <code>--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox</code> 启动。预期结果：Codex 会拒绝启动，或强制使用兼容的权限配置文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 使用 <code>--sandbox danger-full-access --ask-for-approval never</code> 启动。预期结果：启动会被拒绝。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 执行普通工作区任务。预期结果：任务成功；跨越边界的操作仍需审批。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"roll-back_1\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_14\">回滚</h4> <p>恢复先前的 requirements 配置包。除非另有单独批准的例外，否则应继续阻止不受限制的访问。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries_1\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_15\">适用边界</h4> <p>此方案控制 Codex 的运行时权限，但不会降低操作系统特权账户本身的权限。</p>  <p><a id=\"allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"mcp\">仅允许已批准的 MCP 服务器</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果组织需要决定 Codex 可以使用哪些 MCP 服务器，并强制执行已批准的服务器清单，请使用此方案。</p> <p>允许服务器接入和配置服务器是两个不同步骤。允许列表中的条目只表示符合条件的服务器可以使用，并不会自动创建服务器配置。你必须另行配置服务器，确保名称和身份标识匹配，并设置其所需的身份验证方式。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>Codex 只会启用名称和身份标识均与企业批准清单相符的 MCP 服务器。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure_2\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_16\">配置</h4> <ol> <li> <p>盘点每台已批准服务器的信息：负责人、用途、传输方式、确切的可执行文件或 URL、身份验证方式、数据分类，以及具有写入能力的工具。</p> </li> <li> <p>先设置为全部拒绝。空的受管表会禁用所有 MCP 服务器：</p> </li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [mcp_servers]\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>使用确切 URL 添加已批准的 HTTP 服务器：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [mcp_servers.internal_docs]\n   identity = { url = \"https://mcp.example.com/docs\" }\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>使用可执行文件和位置参数添加已批准的 stdio 服务器：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [mcp_servers.internal_build.identity]\n   command = { executable = \"/usr/local/bin/company-mcp\", args = [\n     { match = \"exact\", value = \"serve\" },\n     { match = \"prefix\", value = \"--workspace=\" },\n   ] }\n</code></pre> <p>结构化匹配器会检查可执行文件、参数数量和参数顺序，但不会限制 <code>cwd</code>、<code>env</code> 或 <code>env_vars</code>。批准 stdio 服务器前，必须先控制完整的启动上下文：保护其可执行文件及依赖项，并阻止不可信的用户或项目配置提供可能改变其行为的工作目录或环境。如果无法独立强制执行这一边界，就不要批准该 stdio 服务器，改用经过审核的 HTTP 服务器。</p> <ol> <li> <p>在 <code>plugins.&lt;plugin&gt;.mcp_servers.&lt;server&gt;</code> 下单独添加插件捆绑的 MCP 身份标识。</p> </li> <li> <p>将批准清单保存在经过审核的配置代码库中，并通过管理员强制执行的 requirements 下发。</p> </li> </ol> <p>只有服务器名称和身份标识都与批准条目匹配时，客户端才会启用该服务器。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"verify_2\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_17\">验证</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 完全按照批准的配置设置服务器。预期结果：服务器启动。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 更改 URL 中的一个字符。预期结果：服务器被禁用。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 更改 stdio 参数的数量或顺序，或提供不符合已配置参数规则的参数。预期结果：服务器被禁用。仍符合允许前缀的值依然会被允许。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 在可丢弃的可信项目中，保持可执行文件和参数匹配，同时尝试无害的 <code>cwd</code>、<code>env</code> 和 <code>env_vars</code> 覆盖。仅靠身份标识匹配并不会拒绝这些覆盖。请验证独立的启动上下文控制会拒绝不安全的覆盖，或者确认该 stdio 服务器仍未获批准；不要使用真实的注入载荷。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 添加一个未批准的服务器名称。预期结果：该服务器被禁用。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 测试插件捆绑的服务器。预期结果：只有身份标识获批准时，服务器才能正常工作。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"roll-back_2\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_18\">回滚</h4> <p>移除刚添加的条目。如需立即停止所有 MCP 使用，请下发空的 <code>mcp_servers</code> 表。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries_2\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_19\">适用边界</h4> <p>身份标识允许列表并不能证明服务器的工具、上游数据、凭据或启动环境是安全的。本地 stdio MCP 进程运行在本地命令沙盒之外；匹配可执行文件和参数并不会限制服务器的访问权限，也无法阻止它通过环境加载代码。请独立审核完整启动上下文和服务器权限。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-command-rules-from-requirements\">MCP 身份要求</a>。</p>  <p><a id=\"monitor-codex-activity\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex_1\">监控 Codex 活动</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-monitor-codex-activity\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果安全团队需要了解 Codex 在终端和网络中的活动，请使用此方案。它会整合运行与工具事件、审批决定、工作区审计记录和网络日志，便于调查。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>安全团队可以关联 Codex 运行记录、工具决定、审批、工作区审计记录和实际网络出站流量，而不必依赖模型自己的叙述。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"use-four-evidence-sources\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_20\">使用四类证据来源</h4> <ol> <li> <p>Codex OpenTelemetry：运行、API、模型、沙盒、审批和工具事件。</p> </li> <li> <p>受管生命周期钩子：围绕工具使用和权限请求记录终端侧事件。</p> </li> <li> <p>网络代理或网关日志：实际 DNS/HTTP 目标及连接结果。</p> </li> <li> <p>Compliance API：工作区级别的审计和调查记录。</p> </li> </ol> <p>任何单一来源都无法提供完整的网络监控信息。</p> <p><a id=\"configure_3\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_21\">配置</h4> <ol> <li>将 Codex OpenTelemetry 日志发送到收集器，并对原始提示词进行脱敏：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [otel]\n   environment = \"enterprise\"\n   log_user_prompt = false\n\n   [otel.exporter.otlp-http]\n   endpoint = \"https://otel.example.com/v1/logs\"\n   protocol = \"binary\"\n   headers = { \"x-otlp-api-key\" = \"${OTLP_TOKEN}\" }\n</code></pre> <p>启动客户端前，通过已批准的机密管理器或进程环境向 Codex 进程提供 <code>OTLP_TOKEN</code>。不要将此令牌写入部署的 TOML 文件或源代码管理系统。</p> <p>Codex 可以发出对话启动、API 请求、流式事件、提示词元数据、工具决定和工具结果等事件。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85\">高级配置</a>。</p> <ol> <li> <p>在项目本地配置之外部署 OTel。项目中的 <code>.codex/config.toml</code> 文件无法覆盖遥测路由设置。</p> </li> <li> <p>为 <code>SessionStart</code>、<code>PreToolUse</code>、<code>PermissionRequest</code>、<code>PostToolUse</code>、<code>Stop</code> 和 <code>SessionEnd</code> 配置受管生命周期钩子。</p> </li> <li> <p>将钩子脚本安装在管理员管理的目录中。如果要忽略用户、项目和插件钩子，请设置 <code>allow_managed_hooks_only = true</code>。</p> </li> <li> <p>通过具有独立日志的代理转发沙盒命令流量。OTel 会记录某个工具已运行；代理则记录子进程尝试连接的目标。</p> </li> <li> <p>为网络搜索、应用、连接器、MCP 服务器、浏览器活动和 Codex Cloud 分别配置遥测。针对本地命令的代理规则不会自动覆盖这些活动。</p> </li> <li> <p>将受支持的 Compliance API 记录传入 SIEM，并自行设置保留和法律保全控制。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-9d54fab7ad7294d0\">Compliance API 和审计事件</a>。</p> </li> <li> <p>将用户或服务帐户、主机或运行器、对话 ID、模型、客户端版本、权限配置、审批决定、目标位置和工具名称关联起来。</p> </li> </ol> <p><a id=\"verify_3\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_22\">验证</h4> <p>运行一次受控会话，执行以下操作：</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 读取一个允许访问的文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试读取一个已阻止访问的文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 运行一个允许的命令。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试发起一个已阻止的出站请求。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 请求审批并收到拒绝结果。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 结束会话。</p> </li> </ul> <p>各证据来源的预期结果：</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] OTel 显示运行和工具事件。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 钩子显示会话生命周期。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 代理日志显示允许和已阻止的目标。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 合规数据接收端收到受支持的记录。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] SIEM 将这些记录与用户或工作负载关联起来。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"roll-back_3\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_23\">回滚</h4> <p>恢复先前配置，以停用导出器和钩子。回滚遥测配置时，不要同时移除独立的代理日志记录。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries_3\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_24\">适用边界</h4> <p>不要承诺 Codex 遥测会捕获每个命令参数、每个输出字节或每个网络数据包。请记录各层的实际覆盖范围。</p>  <p><a id=\"restrict-network-destinations\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_25\">限制网络目标</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-restrict-network-destinations\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果你希望 Codex 无法访问互联网，或只能访问已批准的目标，请使用此方案。请根据所配置的环境选择本地或云端流程，并注意它们各自适用的策略范围。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>根据所选配置，Codex 无法访问互联网，或只能访问已批准的域名。Codex Cloud（Legacy）环境还可以进一步限制 HTTP 方法。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure-current-codex-cloud\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"codex-cloud\">配置当前版 Codex Cloud</h4> <ol> <li> <p>为不同信任级别创建独立环境，例如无互联网访问的审查环境、允许下载依赖项的构建环境，以及网络安全实验环境。</p> </li> <li> <p>在环境配置中，除非工作流程确实需要互联网访问，否则请关闭 <strong>Allow Codex to access internet</strong>。</p> </li> <li> <p>如果确实需要访问互联网，请开启 <strong>Allow Codex to access internet</strong>。在 <strong>Allow domains</strong> 下选择 <strong>Custom domains only</strong>，然后在 <strong>Additional allowed domains</strong> 下添加确切需要访问的主机。</p> </li> <li> <p>设置期间保存配置并测试相关服务。发布或重新发布环境，然后在新任务中测试对已批准和未批准目标的访问。请单独审核设置脚本：固定依赖项版本，优先使用已批准的镜像，并避免下载未经审核的可执行文件。</p> </li> </ol> <p>请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">连接到服务</a>。当前版 Codex Cloud 设置不提供 Legacy 中的 HTTP 方法选择器。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-codex-cloud-legacy\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"codex-cloudlegacy\">配置 Codex Cloud（Legacy）</h4> <ol> <li> <p>如果不需要互联网访问，请保持代理阶段的互联网访问 <strong>Off</strong>。Legacy Cloud 默认会在代理阶段阻止互联网访问；但设置脚本仍可访问互联网以安装依赖项。</p> </li> <li> <p>如果代理确实需要访问互联网，请开启该选项并使用范围严格的允许列表。先选择 <strong>None</strong>，再添加确切的域名。</p> </li> <li> <p>对于仅需下载内容的工作流程，只允许 <code>GET</code>、<code>HEAD</code> 和 <code>OPTIONS</code>。这样会阻止 <code>POST</code>、<code>PUT</code>、<code>PATCH</code>、<code>DELETE</code> 和其他方法。请单独审核设置脚本，因为代理阶段的限制不适用于设置脚本的互联网访问。</p> </li> </ol> <p>请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">Legacy 代理互联网访问</a>。这些预设和 HTTP 方法控制仅适用于 Legacy Cloud。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-local-codex\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"codex_2\">配置本地 Codex</h4> <ol> <li>在管理员管理的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中定义并选择已批准的网络配置文件，并限制用户只能使用该配置文件：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   default_permissions = \"corp-build\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \"corp-build\" = true\n\n   [permissions.corp-build]\n   description = \"Workspace access with approved dependency destinations.\"\n   extends = \":workspace\"\n\n   [permissions.corp-build.network]\n   enabled = true\n   allow_local_binding = false\n\n   [permissions.corp-build.network.domains]\n   \"registry.npmjs.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"pypi.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"files.pythonhosted.org\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>使用<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>部署经过审核的 requirements 配置包。仅定义命名配置文件并不会使其生效；必须由 <code>default_permissions</code> 选中该配置文件，并通过允许列表阻止用户选择其他配置文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>在同一 requirements 配置包中启用管理员管理的代理，以强制执行域名限制并阻止用户扩大限制范围。请同时部署代理和启用网络的配置文件；如果启用了网络的配置文件没有活动代理，就会授予直接网络访问权限，而不会执行域名规则：</p> </li> </ol> <blockquote> <p><strong>注意：</strong> <code>[experimental_network]</code> 属于实验性功能，后续可能发生变化。大范围部署前，请在每个受支持的本地客户端版本和操作系统上验证。Windows 支持有限；在你的环境中测试并确认策略确实生效前，不要向 Windows 用户应用此策略。</p> </blockquote> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [experimental_network]\n   enabled = true\n   managed_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n   [experimental_network.domains]\n   \"registry.npmjs.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"pypi.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"files.pythonhosted.org\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>除非工作流程确实需要，否则请关闭本地端口绑定。请单独配置对私有网络目标的访问；允许某个域名并不代表同时批准该域名解析到的私有地址。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">网络访问要求</a>。</p> </li> <li> <p>分别配置网络搜索、应用、MCP 服务器、浏览器活动和 Codex Cloud。本地沙盒规则不会控制这些功能。</p> </li> </ol> <p><a id=\"verify_4\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_26\">验证</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 向已批准的域名发送请求，并确认请求成功。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 向未批准的域名发送请求，并确认请求被阻止。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 分别向 <a href=\"http://localhost\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">localhost（需联网）</a>、私有 IP 和云元数据端点发送请求。确认除非得到明确批准，否则每个请求都会被阻止。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 在 Codex Cloud（Legacy）中，尝试向已允许的域名发送 <code>POST</code> 请求。确认仅允许 <code>GET</code>、<code>HEAD</code> 和 <code>OPTIONS</code> 时，该请求会被阻止。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 确认代理或网关日志中同时记录了允许和拒绝的请求。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"roll-back_4\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_27\">回滚</h4> <p>将环境恢复为 Off，或还原先前的域名策略。避免回滚到不受限制的互联网访问。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries_4\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_28\">适用边界</h4> <p>获准的完全沙盒权限提升可能绕过本地命令代理。依赖此限制之前，请分别测试普通沙盒命令和已批准的提升权限命令。</p> <p>已允许的域名仍可能托管提示词注入内容、受入侵的软件包或不安全的文档。</p>  <p><a id=\"protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_29\">保护机密信息及无关代码库</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果 Codex 需要访问已批准的源代码目录，同时必须阻止其访问机密信息、<code>.env</code> 文件、SSH 密钥和无关代码库，请使用此方案。方案内容包括限制文件访问，以及减少传递给启动命令的凭据类环境变量数量。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>Codex 可以在已批准的源代码目录中工作，但无法读取常见的机密文件，也不会继承大量包含凭据的环境变量。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure_4\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_30\">配置</h4> <ol> <li>在工作区权限配置文件中定义拒绝读取的匹配模式：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [permissions.corp-workspace]\n   description = \"Workspace edits with secret-bearing paths denied.\"\n   extends = \":workspace\"\n\n   [permissions.corp-workspace.filesystem]\n   glob_scan_max_depth = 6\n\n   [permissions.corp-workspace.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n   \"**/.env*\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/*.pem\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/*.p12\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/id_*\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/.aws/**\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/.kube/**\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>为已知的凭据目录、签名密钥、生产配置和相邻代码库添加明确的拒绝路径。</p> </li> <li> <p>在系统级 <code>/etc/codex/config.toml</code> 或用户级 <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> 中，减少传递给启动命令的环境变量：</p> </li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [shell_environment_policy]\n   inherit = \"core\"\n   ignore_default_excludes = false\n\n   [shell_environment_policy.filters]\n   \"AWS_*\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"AZURE_*\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"GITHUB_*\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"*_TOKEN\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"*_SECRET\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"*_PASSWORD\" = \"exclude\"\n</code></pre> <p>这是用户可配置的默认值，并非强制要求。它可以减少继承的环境变量，但不能阻止访问凭据文件、辅助程序、Shell 启动内容或其他身份。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85\">高级配置</a>。</p> <ol> <li>在管理员管理的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中选择受限配置文件，并只允许使用该配置文件。内置配置文件不会继承其中的机密文件拒绝规则：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   default_permissions = \"corp-workspace\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \"corp-workspace\" = true\n   \":danger-full-access\" = false\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>为 Cloud 环境选择凭据流程：</li> <li>在当前版 Codex Cloud 中，对于要发送到已允许 HTTPS 服务的凭据，请使用 <strong>Network secrets</strong>。程序会收到一个占位符；在设置阶段和任务执行期间，代理会针对 443 端口上的已批准目标将其替换为真实值。原始凭据不会放入本地进程或文件中。程序可以使用直接环境变量。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=configure-environment-variables-and-network-secrets\">环境变量和网络机密</a>。</li> <li> <p>在 Codex Cloud（Legacy）中，机密信息用于设置阶段的身份验证，并会在代理阶段开始前移除；环境变量则会在整个聊天期间保留。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-73011d6801bc30d3\">Legacy Cloud 环境</a>。</p> </li> <li> <p>如果代理需要身份验证，请使用独立的凭据代理，提供仅对确切目标和操作有效的短期凭据。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b74b7c76c711106d\">建议配置</a>。</p> </li> </ol> <p><a id=\"verify_5\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_31\">验证</h4> <p>创建无害的诱饵文件：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">.env.test\ntest-key.pem\n.aws/credentials\n.kube/config\n</code></pre> <p>然后要求 Codex 执行以下操作：</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 读取一个普通文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 读取每个诱饵文件。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 递归搜索机密信息。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 输出命令环境变量。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 读取一个相邻代码库。</p> </li> </ul> <p>预期结果：可以成功读取普通文件；被拒绝的文件和相邻代码库仍无法访问；命令环境中不包含已排除的变量。</p> <p><a id=\"roll-back_5\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_32\">回滚</h4> <p>恢复先前的配置文件，但在调查工作流程故障期间，应保留高度可信的机密文件拒绝规则。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries_5\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_33\">适用边界</h4> <p>文件系统拒绝规则不会清除已粘贴到提示词中、已提交到可读取源代码中、由连接器返回或写入模型可访问日志的机密信息。</p>  <p><a id=\"require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_34\">要求敏感命令经过审批</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>如果 <code>git push</code>、部署、数据库迁移和云 CLI 命令等操作必须先经审批才能执行，请使用此方案。确定哪些命令需要提示审核、哪些命令必须禁止，然后为这些命令配置规则。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>预期结果</strong></p> <p>即使模型认为操作合适，敏感命令也会始终暂停并等待审批，或直接被禁止。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure_5\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_35\">配置</h4> <ol> <li>确定哪些命令需要提示审批，哪些命令必须禁止。</li> </ol> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>命令类别</th> <th>处理方式</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>git push</code>、<code>git commit</code></td> <td>提示审批</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>terraform apply</code></td> <td>提示审批</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>kubectl apply/delete/patch</code></td> <td>提示审批</td> </tr> <tr> <td>数据库迁移</td> <td>提示审批</td> </tr> <tr> <td>破坏性主机命令</td> <td>禁止</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <ol> <li>添加管理员强制执行的命令规则：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [rules]\n   prefix_rules = [\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { any_of = [\"push\", \"commit\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before mutating shared Git state.\" },\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"terraform\" }, { token = \"apply\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before infrastructure changes.\" },\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"kubectl\" }, { any_of = [\"apply\", \"delete\", \"patch\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before Kubernetes changes.\" },\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"rm\" }], decision = \"forbidden\", justification = \"Destructive removal is not permitted in managed Codex workflows.\" },\n   ]\n</code></pre> <p>Requirements 规则可以要求命令先经提示审批，或直接禁止命令；但不能用来允许命令。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>。</p> <ol> <li>对于高风险配置文件，如果 Shell 包装器可能隐藏敏感命令，请在同一 <code>prefix_rules</code> 数组中添加 Shell 包装器条目：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   { pattern = [{ any_of = [\"bash\", \"sh\", \"zsh\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review shell wrappers that could obscure sensitive commands.\" },\n</code></pre> <p>Shell 包装器条目会产生较多提示，请有选择地使用。</p> <ol> <li>如果命令必须由真人审批，请强制执行以下设置：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\", \"untrusted\"]\n   allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Auto-review 改变的是审核者，并非确定性的安全保证。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">Auto-review</a>。</p> <ol> <li>将 PermissionRequest、工具决定和工具结果事件发送到安全处理流程。</li> </ol> <p><a id=\"verify_6\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_36\">验证</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 运行 <code>git status</code>。预期结果：不会出现审批提示。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 运行 <code>git push</code>。预期结果：出现审批提示。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 运行 <code>terraform apply</code>。预期结果：出现审批提示。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 运行 <code>kubectl delete pod test</code>。预期结果：出现审批提示。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 运行 <code>rm -rf test-directory</code>。预期结果：该命令被禁止。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 拒绝一次审批请求。预期结果：命令不会运行，拒绝结果会写入日志。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 重启客户端并重复检查。预期结果：规则仍然生效。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"roll-back_6\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_37\">回滚</h4> <p>恢复先前的 requirements 配置包。如果令牌匹配模式过于宽泛，应缩小匹配范围，而不是移除整个敏感命令类别。</p> <p><a id=\"boundaries_6\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_38\">适用边界</h4> <p>前缀规则并非完整的操作系统策略引擎。请将其与沙盒、遵循最小权限原则的操作系统账户、网络限制、云 IAM、分支保护、部署审批和数据库授权配合使用。</p>  <p><a id=\"combined-recipes-for-enterprise-security-goals\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_39\">企业安全目标的组合方案</h2> <p>以下示例展示如何组合控制措施，以实现常见的企业安全目标。请在范围内的每种客户端和环境中测试配置。</p> <p><a id=\"let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex_3\">在保护生产环境的同时让开发者使用 Codex</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p><strong>使用以下配置方案：</strong> 限制权限配置文件和完全访问权限；保护机密信息及无关代码库；要求敏感命令经过审批。</p> <p>开发者拥有正当的生产环境访问权限，但你希望 Codex 能协助开发，同时不让它未经审核就更改生产环境。</p> <ul> <li> <p>定义已批准的工作区配置文件，并强制执行允许的配置文件和审批策略。将这些设置作为管理员强制要求下发，防止用户覆盖。</p> </li> <li> <p>让 Codex 使用独立且权限较低的身份运行，或在无法访问和获取生产写入凭据的环境中运行。开发者仍可通过单独授权的工作流程访问生产环境。</p> </li> <li> <p>尽量减少传递给启动命令的环境变量。如果代理需要身份验证，请使用独立的凭据代理，提供仅对确切目标和操作有效的短期凭据。</p> </li> <li> <p>确定哪些命令需要提示审批，哪些命令必须禁止。要求真人审批选定的高影响操作，例如 <code>git push</code>、<code>terraform apply</code>、Kubernetes 更改和数据库迁移；禁止绝不能运行的命令。</p> </li> <li> <p>独立于 Codex 强制执行生产 IAM、分支保护和部署审批。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"restrict-permission-profiles-and-require-a-human-reviewer\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_40\">限制权限配置文件，并要求真人审核</h4> <p>在管理员管理的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中允许内置的工作区和只读配置文件，并要求需要审批的操作由用户审核：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\ndefault_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>工作区配置文件允许在沙盒范围内操作工作区；只读配置文件则适用于权限要求更窄的工作。完全访问配置文件不会加入允许列表。</p> <p>如果客户端需要明确指定兼容设置，请在客户端配置中设置 <code>approval_policy = \"on-request\"</code>。<code>on-request</code> 会在需要时允许出现审批提示，不会对每条命令都提示。请为需要审核的操作添加明确规则，并保留独立的生产环境授权。</p> <p>受管配置文件允许列表要求 Codex 0.138.0 或更高版本；对于较旧的客户端，请使用并测试文档中说明的旧版沙盒模式要求。</p> <p>重启每个试点客户端，确认已批准的配置文件可以正常工作，并确认未获允许的完全访问配置文件会被拒绝。</p> <p><a id=\"separate-the-codex-identity-from-production-credentials\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"codex_4\">将 Codex 身份与生产凭据分开</h4> <p>在设备管理或开发环境配置系统中，创建专用的 Codex 用户、容器或开发主机，使其只能访问所需的源代码和开发服务。不要将开发者的生产凭据文件、令牌辅助工具或密钥链挂载到该环境中，并阻止该身份获得生产写入角色。</p> <p>如果代理需要访问服务，请配置组织的凭据代理，使其只签发适用于开发资源和操作的短期凭据。开发者可以通过单独授权的生产环境工作流程使用自己的常规身份。</p> <p>例如，在 AWS IAM 中打开 <strong>Roles</strong>，选择 Codex 开发角色，然后在 <strong>Permissions</strong> 下添加经过审核的内联策略，例如以下示例；请将账户和角色替换为你自己的值：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"DenyAssumeExampleProductionRole\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/ExampleProductionRole\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n</code></pre> <p>此示例会拒绝对指定角色的 AssumeRole 请求，但不会拒绝对其他角色的请求。还应移除直接生产权限、限制其他生产角色和凭据来源，并审核信任策略和组织级控制措施。</p> <p>不要实际更改生产环境；请使用有代表性的开发和生产权限测试开发角色。请参阅 <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_create_policy-examples.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AWS IAM 角色策略示例（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"minimize-the-shell-environment\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"shell\">尽量缩小 Shell 环境变量范围</h4> <p>在系统级初始配置 <code>/etc/codex/config.toml</code> 或用户级配置 <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> 中，设置启动命令可以继承哪些环境变量：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[shell_environment_policy]\ninherit = \"core\"\nignore_default_excludes = false\n\n[shell_environment_policy.filters]\n\"AWS_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"AZURE_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"GITHUB_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_TOKEN\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_SECRET\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_PASSWORD\" = \"exclude\"\n</code></pre> <p>这是可配置的默认值，不是强制执行的凭据边界。用户设置、Shell 启动内容或快照、凭据辅助工具以及其他凭据来源仍可能暴露凭据。</p> <p>如果符合你的工作流程，可以在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中使用以下可选限制，禁用登录 Shell 和 Shell 快照：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_login_shell = false\n\n[features]\nshell_snapshot = false\n</code></pre> <p>请先试点这些设置，因为某些工作流程依赖登录 Shell 的初始化。</p> <p>使用与过滤规则匹配的无害模拟变量启动 Codex，并检查启动命令的环境；如果已禁用登录 Shell，也要确认登录 Shell 请求会被拒绝。另行验证执行身份无法访问真实的生产凭据。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=shell-environment-policy\">Shell 环境策略</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"prompt-for-selected-high-impact-commands\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_41\">对选定的高影响命令提示审批</h4> <p>为组织实际使用的命令添加 requirements 规则。例如：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { token = \"push\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review Git pushes.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"terraform\" }, { token = \"apply\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review infrastructure changes.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"kubectl\" }, { any_of = [\"apply\", \"delete\", \"patch\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review cluster changes.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"python3\" }, { token = \"manage.py\" }, { token = \"migrate\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review database migrations.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>对绝不能运行的命令使用 <code>forbidden</code>；请根据实际部署和迁移工具替换或添加匹配模式。</p> <p>这些规则匹配命令前缀，并不能覆盖所有等效操作。请分别测试绝对路径、包装器、其他参数顺序、其他客户端和其他工具。</p> <p>在可丢弃的测试目标上运行普通命令、请求执行列表中的操作、拒绝审批请求，并确认该操作没有执行。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-command-rules-from-requirements\">强制执行命令规则</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"enforce-repository-and-deployment-approvals-outside-codex\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"codex_5\">在 Codex 之外强制执行代码库和部署审批</h4> <p>在源代码管理平台中保护 <code>main</code> 等生产分支：要求变更经过审核，并满足必要的审批和状态检查；同时审核哪些角色可以绕过这些要求。在相应部署系统中配置部署审批，并直接在数据库中设置数据库权限。</p> <p>使用可丢弃的代码库验证经过审核的变更可以正常进行，并确认未经授权的直接推送会被拒绝。无论操作是否由 Codex 发起，这些控制措施都会生效。</p> <p><a id=\"verify_7\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_42\">验证</h4> <p>在可丢弃的测试目标上：</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 执行普通工作区任务。预期结果：任务成功。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试执行一个有代表性的敏感命令，并拒绝审批请求。预期结果：被拒绝的命令不会运行。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 验证 Codex 身份无法获得生产环境写入权限。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 重启客户端并重复检查。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"boundaries_7\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_43\">适用边界</h4> <p>前缀规则并非完整的操作系统策略引擎。请将其与沙盒、遵循最小权限原则的操作系统账户、网络限制、云 IAM、分支保护、部署审批和数据库授权配合使用。</p>  <p><a id=\"reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_44\">降低内部代码被公开发布的风险</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p><strong>使用以下配置方案：</strong> 保护机密信息及无关代码库；限制网络目标；仅允许已批准的 MCP 服务器；要求敏感命令经过审批。</p> <p>开发者会使用 Codex 处理内部代码库，而你希望降低代码被发送到公共代码库、公开代码片段或其他未批准目标的风险。</p> <ul> <li> <p>为已知凭据目录和无关代码库添加明确的拒绝路径，并尽量减少传递给启动命令的环境变量。</p> </li> <li> <p>如果代理需要访问互联网，请使用范围严格的已批准域名允许列表。只允许已批准的 MCP 服务器名称和身份标识，并要求审核选定的发布命令（例如 <code>git push</code>），或直接禁止这些命令。</p> </li> <li> <p>使用源代码管理钩子和权限、针对公共代码库和代码片段的策略，以及符合组织需要的数据丢失防护控制。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"deny-access-to-unrelated-repositories-and-credential-files\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_45\">拒绝访问无关代码库和凭据文件</h4> <p>在管理员管理的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中添加本地代理不得读取的明确路径：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.filesystem]\ndeny_read = [\"~/.ssh\", \"~/.aws/credentials\", \"~/.kube/config\", \"/work/unrelated-repository\"]\n</code></pre> <p>将示例替换为受管设备上实际存在的路径。这项受管拒绝规则适用于所有允许的权限配置文件；如果拒绝规则只配置在某个自定义配置文件中，就无法保护选择其他配置文件的用户。优先使用明确已知的路径，并测试所需目录深度下的 glob 匹配模式。</p> <p>在原生 Windows 上，受管 <code>deny_read</code> 会覆盖直接文件工具，但单靠它无法阻止 Shell 子进程读取文件；还应使用操作系统身份和文件访问控制列表。</p> <p>在拒绝访问的位置创建无害的诱饵文件。在每个允许的配置文件下，验证直接读取和通过 Shell 读取这些文件都会被拒绝，同时确认已批准的源文件可以读取。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-deny-read-requirements\">受管 deny-read 要求</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"minimize-credential-bearing-shell-variables\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"shell_1\">尽量减少包含凭据的 Shell 变量</h4> <p>在 <code>/etc/codex/config.toml</code> 或 <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> 中设置范围严格的环境变量继承规则：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[shell_environment_policy]\ninherit = \"core\"\nignore_default_excludes = false\n\n[shell_environment_policy.filters]\n\"AWS_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"AZURE_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"GITHUB_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_TOKEN\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_SECRET\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_PASSWORD\" = \"exclude\"\n</code></pre> <p>此设置可以减少继承的变量，但可由用户自行配置，也无法阻止访问凭据文件、辅助程序、Shell 启动内容或其他身份。</p> <p>在适当情况下，管理员也可以将这些限制添加到 <code>requirements.toml</code>，并先进行试点，以评估它们对开发者工作流程的影响：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_login_shell = false\n\n[features]\nshell_snapshot = false\n</code></pre> <p>使用模拟的诱饵变量和独立的最小权限执行身份进行测试。不要使用真实凭据。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=shell-environment-policy\">Shell 环境策略</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"allow-only-approved-network-destinations-for-local-commands\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_46\">仅允许本地命令访问已批准的网络目标</h4> <p>如果本地沙盒命令需要访问网络，请将下方配置文件和受管代理设置一并合并到 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中，并将域名替换为已批准的端点：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"corp_network\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\ncorp_network = true\n\n[permissions.corp_network]\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.corp_network.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[experimental_network]\nenabled = true\nmanaged_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n[experimental_network.domains]\n\"git.corp.example\" = \"allow\"\n\"registry.corp.example\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>启用网络的配置文件会为沙盒命令提供网络通道，而受管代理会限制其访问目标。请同时部署两者；如果只启用网络配置文件而没有预期的受管代理，就不会执行相同的目标限制。如果不需要互联网访问，请保持网络访问禁用。</p> <p>不要将规范的 <code>domains</code> 表与旧版 <code>allowed_domains</code> 或 <code>denied_domains</code> 设置混用；请迁移并测试现有策略。</p> <p>重启客户端，并验证已批准的端点可以访问、未批准的端点会被阻止。</p> <p>这些设置仍属实验性；请确认操作系统和客户端均受支持。如果已批准的主机解析到私有地址，请另行配置私有网络访问。获准的完全沙盒权限提升可能绕过命令代理。</p> <p>针对本地命令的网络控制本身不会覆盖网络搜索、应用、MCP 服务器、浏览器活动或云环境；这些功能需要单独配置策略。已批准的托管域名也可能包含公共代码库或公开代码片段，因此只允许目标域名并不能保护内容。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">网络访问要求</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-cloud-environment-networking-separately\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_47\">单独配置云环境网络</h4> <p>在当前版 Codex Cloud 界面中打开环境配置。保持 <strong>Allow Codex to access internet</strong> 关闭。如果需要访问互联网，请启用该选项并选择 <strong>Custom domains only</strong>，在 <strong>Additional allowed domains</strong> 下添加确切需要访问的主机，然后保存配置、发布或重新发布环境，并测试一个新任务。</p> <p>请独立审核服务身份验证和功能权限。</p> <p>Legacy Codex Cloud 使用按环境单独设置的代理互联网访问选项，并提供域名和 HTTP 方法控制；设置脚本有其自身的访问行为。</p> <p>请测试你实际使用的环境版本。参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">连接到服务</a>和 <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">Legacy 互联网设置</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"restrict-mcp-servers-and-prompt-before-git-pushes\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"mcp-git\">限制 MCP 服务器，并在 Git 推送前提示审批</h4> <p>在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中只允许已批准的本地 MCP 服务器身份标识。例如：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.internal]\nidentity = { url = \"https://mcp.corp.example/mcp\" }\n</code></pre> <p>在用户级或受管默认值 <code>config.toml</code> 中单独配置实际服务器，使用匹配的名称和 URL，并设置服务器所需的身份验证方式：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.internal]\nurl = \"https://mcp.corp.example/mcp\"\n</code></pre> <p>该要求只允许匹配的服务器，并不会自动创建服务器。请审核服务器自身的访问权限和工具。</p> <p>验证已批准的服务器可以连接，并确认名称或 URL 不同的服务器不会获准接入。</p> <p>添加受管命令规则，在有代表性的发布命令执行前要求审核：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { token = \"push\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review the destination before publishing.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>如果必须由真人审核，请将此规则与顶层要求 <code>allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]</code> 和 <code>allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]</code> 配合使用。</p> <p>拒绝一次测试推送，并确认它没有执行。</p> <p>前缀匹配无法覆盖所有包装器、API 客户端、应用或其他发布路径。</p> <p><a id=\"restrict-public-publishing-with-source-control-and-dlp-controls\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"dlp\">使用源代码管理和 DLP 控制限制公开发布</h4> <p>如果源代码管理平台支持，请限制创建公共代码库，并限制能够更改代码库可见性的人员。审核特权用户的绕过权限。另行检查个人账户、代码片段、外部组织和令牌访问：组织设置无法管理所有目标。</p> <p>在相关代码库中要求变更经过审核，并检查分支保护和绕过权限。</p> <p>在组织的 DLP 控制台中，为敏感源代码内容以及 DLP 产品实际覆盖的上传或代码库渠道配置试点策略；将处理操作设为阻止，并使用无害的模拟标记进行测试。</p> <p>如果源代码管理平台支持服务器端控制，请为接收推送的代码库配置并强制执行经过审核的钩子。</p> <p>在任何正式发布前，请使用已授权的测试目标，并确认阻止策略确实生效。</p> <p><a id=\"verify_8\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_48\">验证</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 创建无害的诱饵文件，并确认读取已拒绝文件的尝试会失败。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 如果启用了网络访问，请确认已批准的目标可以访问、未批准的目标会被阻止；否则，请验证网络请求会被阻止。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 使用无害标记和可丢弃的测试账户或目标，验证有代表性的公开发布尝试会在发布前被阻止。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"boundaries_8\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_49\">适用边界</h4> <p>已允许的域名仍可能托管公开内容。请分别配置网络搜索、应用、MCP 服务器、浏览器活动和 Codex Cloud；本地沙盒规则不会控制这些功能。</p>  <p><a id=\"keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex_6\">防止 Codex 更改自身的安全防护措施</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p><strong>本方案会用到以下配置方案：</strong> 限制权限配置文件与完全访问权限；仅允许已批准的 MCP 服务器；限制网络目标；保护机密信息及无关代码库；要求敏感命令经过审批。</p> <p>如果你希望 Codex 在代码库中工作时仍受既定限制约束，并且用户修改本地配置也无法绕过这些限制，请使用此方案。</p> <ul> <li> <p>将受支持的权限、审批、MCP、网络和命令限制作为管理员强制执行的 requirements 下发。</p> </li> <li> <p>将钩子脚本安装在管理员管理的目录中；如果要忽略用户、项目和插件级钩子，请启用仅使用受管钩子的设置。</p> </li> <li> <p>使用操作系统和设备管理控制保护策略文件及配置分发路径。运行 Codex 的账户不应拥有修改这些保护措施所需的权限。</p> </li> <li> <p>检查范围内每种客户端和环境实际生效的配置。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"enforce-the-permitted-profiles-approvals-server-identities-and-commands\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_50\">强制执行允许的配置文件、审批者、服务器身份和命令</h4> <p>将审核过的设置合并到管理员管理的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中，不要依赖用户或代码库中可编辑的设置。例如：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\ndefault_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\n\n[mcp_servers.internal]\nidentity = { url = \"https://mcp.corp.example/mcp\" }\n\n[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { token = \"push\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review Git pushes.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>此示例允许两种权限配置文件；需要审批时，要求由真人审核；只允许身份标识匹配的 MCP 服务器；并对特定命令前缀提示审批。</p> <p>还需单独配置 MCP 服务器本身，并添加组织中的其他敏感命令模式。必要时添加全局文件限制和网络限制。requirements 只能约束受支持的设置，不能替代 Codex 之外的身份、服务和目标控制措施。</p> <p>受管权限配置文件允许列表要求 Codex 0.138.0 或更高版本。</p> <p>在试点客户端上，尝试选择未获允许的配置文件、更改受限的用户或项目设置，以及使用不在允许列表中的 MCP 身份标识；逐项确认这些操作会被拒绝，或在重启后仍受到约束。拒绝一个测试命令的审批，并确认该命令不会运行。</p> <p><a id=\"add-managed-filesystem-and-network-restrictions\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_51\">添加受管文件系统与网络限制</h4> <p>如要在所有允许的配置文件中拒绝访问敏感本地文件，请将以下表格添加到 <code>requirements.toml</code>，并把示例路径替换为组织中的实际路径：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.filesystem]\ndeny_read = [\"~/.ssh\", \"~/.aws/credentials\", \"~/.kube/config\"]\n</code></pre> <p>如果沙盒中的本地命令需要访问网络，请同时定义一个获准启用网络的配置文件和受管网络目标：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"corp_network\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\ncorp_network = true\n\n[permissions.corp_network]\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.corp_network.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[experimental_network]\nenabled = true\nmanaged_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n[experimental_network.domains]\n\"git.corp.example\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>可以用此配置文件策略替换前面的 <code>:workspace</code> 默认配置，也可以有意地合并两种配置文件名称；不要粘贴重复的键或表。如果命令不需要网络访问，请继续保持禁用。</p> <p>使用无害的测试文件核实哪些具体路径会被拒绝，并分别验证获准和未获准的网络目标。</p> <p>在原生 Windows 上，还应使用文件访问控制列表；仅靠受管 <code>deny_read</code> 无法限制 Shell 子进程读取文件。</p> <p>本地命令的网络规则不适用于其他客户端、应用、连接器或云端执行环境。</p> <p><a id=\"install-and-enforce-administrator-managed-hooks\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_52\">安装并强制执行管理员管理的钩子</h4> <p>请管理员将经过审核的钩子程序安装在 root 管理的目录中，例如 <code>/etc/codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py</code>，并阻止 Codex 用户修改该文件或其所在目录。在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中启用钩子，并忽略用户、项目、会话和插件级钩子：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_managed_hooks_only = true\n\n[features]\nhooks = true\n\n[hooks]\nmanaged_dir = \"/etc/codex/hooks\"\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\nmatcher = \"^Bash$\"\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]\ntype = \"command\"\ncommand = \"/usr/bin/python3 /etc/codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\"\ntimeout = 30\nstatusMessage = \"Checking managed Bash command\"\n</code></pre> <p>requirements 只会注册钩子，不会安装钩子程序。要在本地 macOS 上进行集成测试，请将以下内容保存为 <code>pre_tool_use_policy.py</code>，再由管理员使用下面的命令安装。</p> <p>该示例只会拦截合成测试标记。在依赖它执行保护前，应将其替换为经过审核、适用于组织的策略。请确认目标设备上存在 <code>/usr/bin/python3</code>；如有必要，调整解释器路径：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import json\nimport sys\n\nevent = json.load(sys.stdin)\ncommand = event.get(\"tool_input\", {}).get(\"command\", \"\")\nif \"CODEX_HOOK_TEST_DENY\" in command:\n    print(\"Synthetic test command blocked by managed hook.\", file=sys.stderr)\n    sys.exit(2)\n</code></pre> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo install -d -o root -g wheel -m 755 /etc/codex/hooks\nsudo install -o root -g wheel -m 644 ./pre_tool_use_policy.py /etc/codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\n</code></pre> <p>重启本地客户端，然后：</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 确认无害的 Bash 命令可以运行。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 确认包含 <code>CODEX_HOOK_TEST_DENY</code> 的命令会被拦截。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 确认 Codex 用户无法修改策略及其依赖项。</p> </li> </ul> <p><code>allow_managed_hooks_only</code> 会跳过所有非受管的用户、项目、会话和插件钩子，并可能影响其他工作流程。</p> <p>Bash 匹配器无法覆盖所有工具，也无法覆盖之后传递给现有进程的所有输入；发生错误、超时或钩子响应格式错误时，工具调用仍可能继续执行。</p> <p>由云端编排的 Work 使用其他受支持的钩子处理程序。请保留独立的操作系统和服务层保护措施。参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-managed-hooks-from-requirements\">受管钩子</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-0aecdb1ef0cec0a4?anchor=pretooluse\">钩子行为</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"protect-the-policy-and-its-distribution\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_53\">保护策略及其分发渠道</h4> <p>在 macOS 上，管理员可以将审核并合并后的 requirements 文件部署到 root 拥有的父目录中，并阻止 Codex 用户写入该文件：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo install -d -o root -g wheel -m 755 /etc/codex\nsudo install -o root -g wheel -m 644 ./requirements.reviewed.toml /etc/codex/requirements.toml\nls -ld /etc/codex\nls -l /etc/codex/requirements.toml\n</code></pre> <p>在 Linux 上，使用适当的管理员组；在 Windows 上，使用管理员管理的访问控制列表保护 ProgramData 目录及文件。限制可以修改 MDM 或云端策略的人员，审核策略变更，并保留可用于回滚的副本。</p> <p>使用非管理员试点账户，验证该账户无法修改策略、父目录或受管钩子程序；再尝试设置较宽松的用户或项目配置，重启 Codex，并确认强制策略仍然有效。</p> <p>请针对范围内的每种客户端和环境分别测试，不要假设所有客户端都会加载相同的策略。</p> <p><a id=\"verify_9\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_54\">验证</h4> <p>使用非管理员测试身份：</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试编辑受管策略。预期结果：受管文件无法更改。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 尝试通过用户或项目配置放宽限制。预期结果：较宽松的设置会被拒绝，或仍受到现有约束。</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] 重启客户端，确认这些限制仍然有效。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"boundaries_9\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_55\">适用边界</h4> <p>拥有足够主机管理员权限的用户或代理可能可以更改周边保护措施。仅依靠用户可编辑的设置，不能替代管理员管理的强制要求。</p>  <p><a id=\"source-pages\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_56\">来源页面</h2> <ul> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85\">高级配置</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">身份验证</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-668fd22585595004\">沙盒</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">自动复核</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">代理审批与安全</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">代理网络访问</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-73011d6801bc30d3\">云环境</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b74b7c76c711106d\">推荐配置</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-9d54fab7ad7294d0\">合规 API 与审计事件</a></p> </li> </ul>","en":"<h1 id=\"codex-security-cookbook\">Codex security cookbook</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote>  <p>Rolling out Codex means deciding which accounts, files, and services it should be able to access and which actions it should be able to take. This cookbook helps enterprise administrators turn those decisions into configuration, test the results, and understand where additional protections are needed.</p> <p>These security recommendations are intended to help you give developers useful access to Codex while reducing the risk of unintended changes, exposure of sensitive data, and changes to the safeguards themselves. Use them to choose controls that fit your workflows and risk requirements, understand their limits, and verify how they work in your environment. They complement the identity, endpoint, network, and service protections your organization already manages.</p> <p>You can start with a specific task—such as restricting sign-in to your company workspace—or a broader goal, such as letting developers use Codex while protecting production.</p> <p>Choose the starting point that fits your work:</p> <ul> <li> <p><strong>Configuration recipes</strong> walk through individual controls, with setup instructions, verification checks, rollback guidance, and limitations.</p> </li> <li> <p><strong>Combined recipes</strong> show how to combine several controls with protections in your identity, source control, deployment, and other systems to meet a common security goal.</p> </li> </ul> <p>The examples give you a starting point to adapt to your organization. Use each configuration example with its accompanying guidance: where it belongs, which clients or environments it applies to, and what it does and does not control.</p> <p>Before a wider rollout, try the configuration on a test endpoint. Check that an intended workflow succeeds and an intentionally restricted action is blocked. Use those results to refine the setup and explain the boundaries to the people who will use Codex.</p> <h2 id=\"choose-your-starting-point\">Choose your starting point</h2> <p>Use the page outline to open a section below.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>If you want to</th> <th>Start with</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Configure one specific control</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=configuration-recipes\">Configuration recipes</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Let developers use Codex while protecting production</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\">Let developers use Codex while protecting production</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Reduce the risk of publishing internal code</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\">Reduce the risk of publishing internal code</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Keep Codex from changing its own safeguards</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\">Keep Codex from changing its own safeguards</a></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The combined recipes include the relevant controls, external protections, and checks for each goal.</p> <h2 id=\"start-here\">Start here</h2> <ol> <li> <p>Inventory the surfaces in scope: desktop app, CLI, IDE extension, Codex Cloud, CI runners, and remote hosts.</p> </li> <li> <p>Pilot the configuration on a test endpoint before a broad rollout.</p> </li> <li> <p>Record the minimum supported Codex version. Permission-profile allowlists require Codex 0.138.0 or later.</p> </li> <li> <p>Keep a rollback copy of each managed configuration bundle.</p> </li> <li> <p>Test one allowed workflow and one intentionally blocked workflow after every change.</p> </li> </ol> <p>Managed requirements are admin-enforced constraints; managed defaults are startup values. Use requirements for supported authentication, sandbox, approval, MCP, plugin, hook, and network restrictions. Authentication requirements must be delivered locally or through macOS MDM; cloud-managed requirements do not enforce them. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> and the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"where-configuration-belongs\">Where configuration belongs</h3> <p>Adapt these example configurations to your organization before rolling them out to a pilot group. The TOML blocks are policy fragments: merge the settings you need into your existing files, without duplicating tables, and replace example names, domains, and paths. The checks describe expected outcomes to validate in your environment.</p> <p>For local Codex, an administrator can deploy enforced requirements in <code>/etc/codex/requirements.toml</code> on macOS and Linux or <code>%ProgramData%\\OpenAI\\Codex\\requirements.toml</code> on Windows. On managed macOS devices, an MDM can deliver the <code>com.openai.codex:requirements_toml_base64</code> preference. Cloud-managed policy can also be configured in the Admin Console under <strong>Agent Security</strong> by selecting a policy, choosing the applicable environment or OS, and configuring <strong>Requirements</strong>. Protect the policy file and its parent directory from modification by the Codex user. Settings in a regular <code>config.toml</code> or managed Defaults provide startup values rather than enforced requirements. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8\">Agent Security</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"configuration-recipes\">Configuration recipes</h2> <p>Choose the control you need. Open a recipe to see its configuration, verification checks, rollback guidance, and limitations. Check items as you verify them; progress is saved in this browser when browser storage is available.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Control</th> <th>Recipe</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Authentication</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\">Restrict sign-in to the company workspace</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Runtime permissions</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\">Restrict permission profiles and Full Access</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>MCP servers</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\">Allow only approved MCP servers</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Monitoring and audit</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=monitor-codex-activity\">Monitor Codex activity</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Networking</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=restrict-network-destinations\">Restrict network destinations</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Files and credentials</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\">Protect secrets and unrelated repositories</a></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Sensitive commands</td> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\">Require approval for sensitive commands</a></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\">Restrict sign-in to the company workspace</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-restrict-sign-in-to-the-company-workspace\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when employees should sign in to Codex through your company’s ChatGPT workspace, with personal ChatGPT accounts and API-key authentication prohibited on managed endpoints.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>On company-managed devices, Codex accepts only ChatGPT sign-in to the company’s ChatGPT workspace.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites</h4> <ul> <li> <p>The UUID of the approved ChatGPT Enterprise workspace.</p> </li> <li> <p>A way to distribute Codex managed configuration to endpoints.</p> </li> <li> <p>A decision to prohibit API-key authentication on managed endpoints.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"configure\">Configure</h4> <ol> <li> <p>Record the approved workspace UUID in your internal configuration repository.</p> </li> <li> <p>Add enforced authentication requirements to the local <code>requirements.toml</code>:</p> </li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_login_methods = [\"chatgpt\"]\n   allowed_chatgpt_workspaces = [\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"]\n</code></pre> <p>The first key permits only ChatGPT authentication; the second restricts sign-in to the approved workspace. Replace the illustrative UUID with your workspace UUID. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713?anchor=requirementstoml\">requirements reference</a>.</p> <ol> <li> <p>Deploy through local <code>requirements.toml</code> or macOS MDM requirements using the paths in <a href=\"#/article/a-649ffe45719449f8?anchor=where-configuration-belongs\">Where configuration belongs</a>. These authentication requirements apply before credentials load and before cloud policy is retrieved; Codex ignores them in cloud-managed requirements. Protect the file and its parent directory from user modification. Legacy <code>managed_config.toml</code> defaults do not enforce this restriction.</p> </li> <li> <p>During the pilot, sign users out or remove previously stored personal Codex sessions. Do not assume a new policy retroactively invalidates an existing session.</p> </li> <li> <p>Pair the login restriction with workspace roles and group access. Authentication controls which identity signs in; RBAC controls which Codex features that identity may use. See <a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Authentication</a>.</p> </li> </ol> <h4 id=\"verify\">Verify</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Sign out of Codex.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Try signing in with a personal ChatGPT account. Expected: Sign-in to the personal workspace is rejected.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Try Codex API-key login. Expected: API-key login is rejected.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Sign in to the approved workspace with the corporate account. Expected: Sign-in succeeds.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Restart the desktop app, CLI, and IDE extension, then repeat.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"roll-back\">Roll back</h4> <p>Restore the previous local authentication requirements and redeploy the reviewed bundle.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries\">Boundaries</h4> <p>This controls managed Codex clients on managed endpoints. Pair it with endpoint application controls and network policy if the requirement is “no personal Codex use from corporate assets.”</p>  <p><a id=\"restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\">Restrict permission profiles and Full Access</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-restrict-permission-profiles-and-full-access\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when developers need read-only or workspace-scoped access, and you want to prevent them from selecting Full Access or bypassing approvals and sandboxing with <code>--yolo</code>.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>Users can select only approved read-only or workspace-scoped profiles. They cannot select <code>danger-full-access</code>, <code>approval_policy = \"never\"</code>, or <code>--yolo</code>.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"configure_1\">Configure</h4> <ol> <li>Define an approved workspace profile:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [permissions.corp-workspace]\n   description = \"Company workspace access with no unrestricted host access.\"\n   extends = \":workspace\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>Enforce the allowed profiles and approval policies:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\", \"untrusted\"]\n   allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\n   default_permissions = \"corp-workspace\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \":read-only\" = true\n   \"corp-workspace\" = true\n   \":danger-full-access\" = false\n</code></pre> <p>The allowlist is complete: profiles omitted from the allowlist or set to false are denied, including profiles added in future versions. See the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a>.</p> <ol> <li>For legacy clients that still use <code>sandbox_mode</code>, use:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\", \"untrusted\"]\n   allowed_sandbox_modes = [\"read-only\", \"workspace-write\"]\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>Deliver this as admin-enforced requirements, not a user-editable config file.</p> </li> <li> <p>If you later permit Auto-review, add it deliberately after testing. Auto-review changes who reviews a boundary crossing; it does not restore a removed boundary. See <a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">Auto-review</a>.</p> </li> </ol> <h4 id=\"verify_1\">Verify</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Open the permissions picker. Expected: Full Access is unavailable or rejected.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Launch with <code>--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox</code>. Expected: Codex rejects the launch or forces a compatible profile.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Launch with <code>--sandbox danger-full-access --ask-for-approval never</code>. Expected: The launch is rejected.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Run a normal workspace task. Expected: The task succeeds; boundary crossings still require approval.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"roll-back_1\">Roll back</h4> <p>Restore the prior requirements bundle. Keep unrestricted access blocked unless there is a separately approved exception.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries_1\">Boundaries</h4> <p>This controls Codex runtime permissions. It does not make a privileged operating-system account unprivileged.</p>  <p><a id=\"allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\">Allow only approved MCP servers</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-allow-only-approved-mcp-servers\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when your organization needs to decide which MCP servers Codex may use and enforce an approved list.</p> <p>Allowing a server and configuring it are separate steps. An allowlist entry permits a matching server; it does not create the server configuration. Configure the server separately with the matching name and identity, along with the authentication it requires.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>Codex enables only MCP servers whose configured name and identity match the enterprise-approved list.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"configure_2\">Configure</h4> <ol> <li> <p>Inventory each approved server: owner, purpose, transport, exact executable or URL, authentication, data classification, and write-capable tools.</p> </li> <li> <p>Start with deny-all. An empty managed table disables all MCP servers:</p> </li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [mcp_servers]\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>Add approved HTTP servers by exact URL:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [mcp_servers.internal_docs]\n   identity = { url = \"https://mcp.example.com/docs\" }\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>Add approved stdio servers by executable and positional arguments:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [mcp_servers.internal_build.identity]\n   command = { executable = \"/usr/local/bin/company-mcp\", args = [\n     { match = \"exact\", value = \"serve\" },\n     { match = \"prefix\", value = \"--workspace=\" },\n   ] }\n</code></pre> <p>The structured matcher checks the executable, argument count, and argument order. It does not constrain <code>cwd</code>, <code>env</code>, or <code>env_vars</code>. Treat control of the full launch context as a prerequisite for approving a stdio server: protect its executable and dependencies, and prevent untrusted user or project configuration from supplying a working directory or environment that can change its behavior. If you cannot enforce that boundary independently, leave the stdio server unapproved and use a reviewed HTTP server instead.</p> <ol> <li> <p>Add plugin-bundled MCP identities separately under <code>plugins.&lt;plugin&gt;.mcp_servers.&lt;server&gt;</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Store the approved list in a reviewed configuration repository and deploy it through admin-enforced requirements.</p> </li> </ol> <p>The client enables a server only when both its name and identity match an approved entry. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"verify_2\">Verify</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Configure the approved server exactly. Expected: The server starts.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Change one URL character. Expected: The server is disabled.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Change the stdio argument count or order, or supply an argument outside the configured argument rules. Expected: The server is disabled. Values that still satisfy an allowed prefix remain permitted.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] In a disposable trusted project, try harmless <code>cwd</code>, <code>env</code>, and <code>env_vars</code> overrides while retaining the matching executable and arguments. Identity matching alone does not reject these overrides. Verify that your independent launch-context control rejects unsafe overrides or that the stdio server remains unapproved; do not use a real injection payload.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Add an unapproved name. Expected: The server is disabled.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Test a plugin-bundled server. Expected: The server works only with the approved identity.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"roll-back_2\">Roll back</h4> <p>Remove the newly added entry. To stop all MCP usage immediately, deploy an empty <code>mcp_servers</code> table.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries_2\">Boundaries</h4> <p>Identity allowlisting does not prove that a server’s tools, upstream data, credentials, or launch environment are safe. Local stdio MCP processes run outside the local command sandbox; matching an executable and arguments does not constrain the server’s access or prevent environment-based code loading. Review the full launch context and server permissions independently. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-command-rules-from-requirements\">MCP identity requirements</a>.</p>  <p><a id=\"monitor-codex-activity\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"monitor-codex-activity\">Monitor Codex activity</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-monitor-codex-activity\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when your security team needs to understand what Codex is doing on endpoints and in your network. It brings together run and tool events, approval decisions, workspace audit records, and network logs for investigation.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>Security teams can correlate Codex runs, tool decisions, approvals, workspace audit records, and actual network egress without relying on the model’s own narrative.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"use-four-evidence-sources\">Use four evidence sources</h4> <ol> <li> <p>Codex OpenTelemetry: run, API, model, sandbox, approval, and tool events.</p> </li> <li> <p>Managed lifecycle hooks: endpoint-side records around tool use and permission requests.</p> </li> <li> <p>Network proxy or gateway logs: actual DNS/HTTP destinations and connection outcomes.</p> </li> <li> <p>Compliance API: workspace-level audit and investigation records.</p> </li> </ol> <p>No single source provides complete network monitoring.</p> <h4 id=\"configure_3\">Configure</h4> <ol> <li>Send Codex OpenTelemetry logs to your collector, with raw prompts redacted:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [otel]\n   environment = \"enterprise\"\n   log_user_prompt = false\n\n   [otel.exporter.otlp-http]\n   endpoint = \"https://otel.example.com/v1/logs\"\n   protocol = \"binary\"\n   headers = { \"x-otlp-api-key\" = \"${OTLP_TOKEN}\" }\n</code></pre> <p>Provide <code>OTLP_TOKEN</code> to the Codex process through your approved secret manager or process environment before starting the client. Keep the token out of deployed TOML files and source control.</p> <p>Codex can emit conversation starts, API requests, streaming events, prompt metadata, tool decisions, and tool results. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85\">Advanced Configuration</a>.</p> <ol> <li> <p>Deploy OTel outside project-local configuration. Project <code>.codex/config.toml</code> files cannot override telemetry routing.</p> </li> <li> <p>Add managed lifecycle hooks for <code>SessionStart</code>, <code>PreToolUse</code>, <code>PermissionRequest</code>, <code>PostToolUse</code>, <code>Stop</code>, and <code>SessionEnd</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Install hook scripts in an administrator-managed directory and set <code>allow_managed_hooks_only = true</code> if user, project, and plugin hooks should be ignored.</p> </li> <li> <p>Route sandboxed command traffic through an independently logged proxy. OTel records that a tool ran; the proxy records where the subprocess attempted to connect.</p> </li> <li> <p>Configure separate telemetry for web search, apps, connectors, MCP servers, browser activity, and Codex Cloud. Local command proxy rules do not automatically cover them.</p> </li> <li> <p>Stream supported Compliance API records into the SIEM and apply your own retention and legal-hold controls. See <a href=\"#/article/a-9d54fab7ad7294d0\">Compliance API and audit events</a>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Correlate user or service account, host or runner, conversation ID, model, client version, permission profile, approval decision, destination, and tool name.</p> </li> </ol> <h4 id=\"verify_3\">Verify</h4> <p>Run a controlled session that does the following:</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Reads one allowed file.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Attempts one blocked file read.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Runs one allowed command.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Attempts one blocked egress request.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Requests approval and receives a denial.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Ends the session.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Expected results by evidence source:</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] OTel shows run and tool events.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Hooks show the session lifecycle.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Proxy logs show allowed and blocked destinations.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Compliance ingestion receives supported records.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] The SIEM correlates those records with the user or workload.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"roll-back_3\">Roll back</h4> <p>Disable the exporter and hooks by restoring the previous configuration. Do not remove independent proxy logging as part of a telemetry rollback.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries_3\">Boundaries</h4> <p>Do not promise that Codex telemetry captures every command argument, output byte, or network packet. Document the coverage of each layer.</p>  <p><a id=\"restrict-network-destinations\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"restrict-network-destinations\">Restrict network destinations</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-restrict-network-destinations\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when Codex should have no internet access or should reach only approved destinations. Choose the local or cloud procedure for the environment you are configuring, and keep their separate policy scopes in mind.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>Codex has no internet access, or reaches only approved domains, according to the configuration you choose. Codex Cloud (Legacy) environments can additionally restrict HTTP methods.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"configure-current-codex-cloud\">Configure current Codex Cloud</h4> <ol> <li> <p>Create separate environments for different trust levels, such as no-internet-review, approved-dependencies-build, and cyber-lab.</p> </li> <li> <p>In the environment configuration, leave <strong>Allow Codex to access internet</strong> off unless the workflow needs internet access.</p> </li> <li> <p>When access is required, turn on <strong>Allow Codex to access internet</strong>. Under <strong>Allow domains</strong>, choose <strong>Custom domains only</strong>, then add exact required hosts under <strong>Additional allowed domains</strong>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Save and test the services during setup. Publish or republish the environment, then test access to approved and unapproved destinations in a new task. Review setup scripts separately: pin dependencies, prefer approved mirrors, and avoid executable downloads that have not been reviewed.</p> </li> </ol> <p>See <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">Connect to services</a>. Current Codex Cloud settings do not provide the Legacy HTTP-method selector.</p> <h4 id=\"configure-codex-cloud-legacy\">Configure Codex Cloud (Legacy)</h4> <ol> <li> <p>Keep agent internet access <strong>Off</strong> when unnecessary. Legacy Cloud blocks internet during the agent phase by default; setup scripts still run with internet access for dependency installation.</p> </li> <li> <p>When agent access is required, turn it <strong>On</strong> with a narrow allowlist. Start with <strong>None</strong> and add exact domains.</p> </li> <li> <p>For download-only workflows, allow only <code>GET</code>, <code>HEAD</code>, and <code>OPTIONS</code>. This blocks <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, and other methods. Review setup scripts separately because the agent-phase restriction does not govern their internet access.</p> </li> </ol> <p>See <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">Legacy agent internet access</a>. These presets and HTTP-method controls apply only to Legacy Cloud.</p> <h4 id=\"configure-local-codex\">Configure local Codex</h4> <ol> <li>In administrator-managed <code>requirements.toml</code>, define and select the approved network profile, and restrict users to that profile:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   default_permissions = \"corp-build\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \"corp-build\" = true\n\n   [permissions.corp-build]\n   description = \"Workspace access with approved dependency destinations.\"\n   extends = \":workspace\"\n\n   [permissions.corp-build.network]\n   enabled = true\n   allow_local_binding = false\n\n   [permissions.corp-build.network.domains]\n   \"registry.npmjs.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"pypi.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"files.pythonhosted.org\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>Deploy the reviewed requirements bundle using <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a>. A named profile alone is inactive; <code>default_permissions</code> selects it, and the allowlist prevents selection of other profiles.</p> </li> <li> <p>Activate the administrator-managed proxy in the same requirements bundle so domain restrictions are enforced and users cannot expand them. Deploy the proxy and network-enabled profile together; a network-enabled profile without an active proxy grants direct network access without enforcing its domain rules:</p> </li> </ol> <blockquote> <p><strong>Caution:</strong> <code>[experimental_network]</code> is experimental and may change. Validate it on each supported local client version and operating system before broader rollout. Windows support is limited; do not apply this policy to Windows users until you have tested enforcement in your environment.</p> </blockquote> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [experimental_network]\n   enabled = true\n   managed_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n   [experimental_network.domains]\n   \"registry.npmjs.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"pypi.org\" = \"allow\"\n   \"files.pythonhosted.org\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>Keep local port binding off unless the workflow needs it. Configure access to private network targets separately; allowing a domain does not by itself approve its private addresses. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">Network access requirements</a>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Configure web search, apps, MCP servers, browser activity, and Codex Cloud separately. Local sandbox rules do not control them.</p> </li> </ol> <h4 id=\"verify_4\">Verify</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Send a request to an approved domain and confirm that it succeeds.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Send a request to an unapproved domain and confirm that it is blocked.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Send requests to <a href=\"http://localhost\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">localhost (online)</a>, a private IP, and a cloud metadata endpoint. Confirm that each request is blocked unless explicitly approved.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] In Codex Cloud (Legacy), attempt a <code>POST</code> request to an allowed domain. Confirm that the request is blocked when only <code>GET</code>, <code>HEAD</code>, and <code>OPTIONS</code> are allowed.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Confirm that proxy or gateway logs contain entries for both allowed and denied requests.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"roll-back_4\">Roll back</h4> <p>Return the environment to Off or restore the prior domain policy. Avoid rolling back to unrestricted internet.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries_4\">Boundaries</h4> <p>An approved full sandbox escalation can bypass the local command proxy. Test ordinary sandboxed commands and approved escalated commands separately before relying on the restriction.</p> <p>An allowed domain can still host prompt injection, compromised packages, or unsafe documentation.</p>  <p><a id=\"protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\">Protect secrets and unrelated repositories</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-protect-secrets-and-unrelated-repositories\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when Codex needs access to approved source directories while secrets, <code>.env</code> files, SSH keys, and unrelated repositories should remain out of reach. It covers restricting file access and reducing the number of credential-bearing environment variables passed to spawned commands.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>Codex can work in approved source directories but cannot read common secret-bearing files or inherit a broad set of credential-bearing environment variables.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"configure_4\">Configure</h4> <ol> <li>Define deny-read patterns in a workspace profile:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [permissions.corp-workspace]\n   description = \"Workspace edits with secret-bearing paths denied.\"\n   extends = \":workspace\"\n\n   [permissions.corp-workspace.filesystem]\n   glob_scan_max_depth = 6\n\n   [permissions.corp-workspace.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n   \"**/.env*\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/*.pem\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/*.p12\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/id_*\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/.aws/**\" = \"deny\"\n   \"**/.kube/**\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>Add exact deny paths for known credential directories, signing keys, production configuration, and neighboring repositories.</p> </li> <li> <p>In the system <code>/etc/codex/config.toml</code> or the user <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, reduce the environment variables passed to spawned commands:</p> </li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [shell_environment_policy]\n   inherit = \"core\"\n   ignore_default_excludes = false\n\n   [shell_environment_policy.filters]\n   \"AWS_*\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"AZURE_*\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"GITHUB_*\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"*_TOKEN\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"*_SECRET\" = \"exclude\"\n   \"*_PASSWORD\" = \"exclude\"\n</code></pre> <p>This is a user-configurable default, not an enforced requirement. It reduces inherited variables but does not prevent access to credential files, helpers, shell startup content, or other identities. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85\">Advanced Configuration</a>.</p> <ol> <li>In the administrator-managed <code>requirements.toml</code>, select the restricted profile and allow only that profile. Built-in profiles do not inherit its secret-file denies:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   default_permissions = \"corp-workspace\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \"corp-workspace\" = true\n   \":danger-full-access\" = false\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>Choose the credential flow for your Cloud environment:</li> <li>In current Codex Cloud, use <strong>Network secrets</strong> for credentials sent to an allowed HTTPS service. Programs receive a placeholder, and the proxy substitutes the real value for approved destinations on port 443 during setup and tasks. The raw credential is not placed in a local process or file. Direct environment variables are available to programs. See <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=configure-environment-variables-and-network-secrets\">Environment variables and network secrets</a>.</li> <li> <p>In Codex Cloud (Legacy), secrets support setup-time authentication and are removed before the agent phase; environment variables remain for the full chat. See <a href=\"#/article/a-73011d6801bc30d3\">Legacy Cloud environments</a>.</p> </li> <li> <p>If the agent needs authentication, use an independent broker to provide short-lived credentials scoped to the exact target and action. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b74b7c76c711106d\">Recommended configuration</a>.</p> </li> </ol> <h4 id=\"verify_5\">Verify</h4> <p>Create harmless canaries:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">.env.test\ntest-key.pem\n.aws/credentials\n.kube/config\n</code></pre> <p>Then ask Codex to:</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Read a normal file.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Read each canary.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Search recursively for secrets.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Print its command environment.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Read a neighboring repository.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Expected: Reading a normal file succeeds; denied files and neighboring repositories remain inaccessible, and excluded variables are absent from the command environment.</p> <h4 id=\"roll-back_5\">Roll back</h4> <p>Restore the prior profile, but retain high-confidence secret-file denies while investigating workflow failures.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries_5\">Boundaries</h4> <p>Filesystem denies do not remove secrets already pasted into prompts, committed to readable source, returned by connectors, or written into model-accessible logs.</p>  <p><a id=\"require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\">Require approval for sensitive commands</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-require-approval-for-sensitive-commands\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p>Use this recipe when actions such as <code>git push</code>, deployments, database migrations, and cloud CLI commands need approval before they run. Decide which commands should prompt for review and which must be forbidden, then configure rules for those commands.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Intended result</strong></p> <p>Sensitive commands consistently pause for approval or are forbidden, even when the model believes they are appropriate.</p> </blockquote> <h4 id=\"configure_5\">Configure</h4> <ol> <li>Decide which commands require a prompt and which are forbidden.</li> </ol> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Command class</th> <th>Decision</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>git push</code>, <code>git commit</code></td> <td>Prompt</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>terraform apply</code></td> <td>Prompt</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>kubectl apply/delete/patch</code></td> <td>Prompt</td> </tr> <tr> <td>database migrations</td> <td>Prompt</td> </tr> <tr> <td>destructive host commands</td> <td>Forbidden</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <ol> <li>Add admin-enforced command rules:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   [rules]\n   prefix_rules = [\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { any_of = [\"push\", \"commit\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before mutating shared Git state.\" },\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"terraform\" }, { token = \"apply\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before infrastructure changes.\" },\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"kubectl\" }, { any_of = [\"apply\", \"delete\", \"patch\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before Kubernetes changes.\" },\n     { pattern = [{ token = \"rm\" }], decision = \"forbidden\", justification = \"Destructive removal is not permitted in managed Codex workflows.\" },\n   ]\n</code></pre> <p>Requirements rules can require a prompt or forbid a command; they cannot allow one. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a>.</p> <ol> <li>For high-risk profiles, add a shell-wrapper entry to the same <code>prefix_rules</code> array when wrappers could obscure a sensitive command:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   { pattern = [{ any_of = [\"bash\", \"sh\", \"zsh\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review shell wrappers that could obscure sensitive commands.\" },\n</code></pre> <p>The shell-wrapper entry is noisy; use it selectively.</p> <ol> <li>If a person must approve commands, enforce these settings:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\", \"untrusted\"]\n   allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Auto-review changes the reviewer; it is not a deterministic security guarantee. See <a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">Auto-review</a>.</p> <ol> <li>Send PermissionRequest, tool-decision, and tool-result events to the security pipeline.</li> </ol> <h4 id=\"verify_6\">Verify</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Run <code>git status</code>. Expected: No approval prompt appears.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Run <code>git push</code>. Expected: An approval prompt appears.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Run <code>terraform apply</code>. Expected: An approval prompt appears.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Run <code>kubectl delete pod test</code>. Expected: An approval prompt appears.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Run <code>rm -rf test-directory</code>. Expected: The command is forbidden.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Deny one approval request. Expected: The command does not run, and the denial is logged.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Restart the client and repeat the checks. Expected: The rule remains enforced.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"roll-back_6\">Roll back</h4> <p>Restore the prior requirements bundle. Narrow an overly broad token pattern instead of removing an entire sensitive command class.</p> <h4 id=\"boundaries_6\">Boundaries</h4> <p>Prefix rules are not a complete operating-system policy engine. Pair them with sandboxing, least-privilege OS accounts, network restrictions, cloud IAM, branch protection, deployment approvals, and database authorization.</p>  <h2 id=\"combined-recipes-for-enterprise-security-goals\">Combined recipes for enterprise security goals</h2> <p>These examples show how to combine controls for common enterprise security goals. Test the configuration on each client and environment in scope.</p> <p><a id=\"let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\">Let developers use Codex while protecting production</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-let-developers-use-codex-while-protecting-production\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p><strong>Uses these configuration recipes:</strong> Restrict permission profiles and Full Access; Protect secrets and unrelated repositories; Require approval for sensitive commands.</p> <p>Developers have legitimate production access, but you want Codex to help with development without making unreviewed production changes.</p> <ul> <li> <p>Define an approved workspace profile and enforce the allowed profiles and approval policies. Deliver these settings as administrator-enforced requirements to prevent users from overriding them.</p> </li> <li> <p>Run Codex under a separate, lower-privilege identity or in an environment that cannot access or obtain production write credentials. Developers can retain production access through a separate authorized workflow.</p> </li> <li> <p>Minimize environment variables passed to spawned commands. If the agent needs authentication, use an independent broker to provide short-lived credentials scoped to the exact target and action.</p> </li> <li> <p>Decide which commands require an approval prompt and which are forbidden. Require a person to approve selected high-impact commands, such as <code>git push</code>, <code>terraform apply</code>, Kubernetes changes, and database migrations; forbid commands that must never run.</p> </li> <li> <p>Keep production IAM, branch protection, and deployment approvals enforced independently of Codex.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"restrict-permission-profiles-and-require-a-human-reviewer\">Restrict permission profiles and require a human reviewer</h4> <p>In the administrator-managed <code>requirements.toml</code>, allow the built-in workspace and read-only profiles and require user review for actions that need approval:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\ndefault_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>The workspace profile permits work in the workspace within its sandbox; the read-only profile is available for narrower work. Full-access profiles are not on the allowlist.</p> <p>Set <code>approval_policy = \"on-request\"</code> in the client configuration if the client needs an explicit compatible choice. <code>on-request</code> permits an approval prompt when required; it does not prompt for every command. Add explicit rules for the actions that need review, and retain independent production authorization.</p> <p>Managed profile allowlists require Codex 0.138.0 or later; use and test the documented legacy sandbox-mode requirements for older clients.</p> <p>Restart each pilot client, verify the approved profile works, and verify a disallowed full-access profile is rejected.</p> <h4 id=\"separate-the-codex-identity-from-production-credentials\">Separate the Codex identity from production credentials</h4> <p>In your device management or development environment provisioning system, create a dedicated Codex user, container, or development host with access only to the source and development services it needs. Do not mount the developer's production credential files, token helpers, or keychains into that environment, and prevent the identity from acquiring production write roles.</p> <p>If the agent needs service access, configure your organization's credential broker to issue short-lived credentials restricted to the development resource and action. The developer can use their normal identity through a separate authorized production workflow.</p> <p>For example, in AWS IAM, open <strong>Roles</strong>, select the Codex development role, and under <strong>Permissions</strong> add a reviewed inline policy like this one, replacing the account and role with your own:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"DenyAssumeExampleProductionRole\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/ExampleProductionRole\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n</code></pre> <p>This example denies attempts to assume the named role, but it does not deny attempts to assume other roles. Also remove direct production permissions, restrict other production roles and credential sources, and review trust policies and organization-level controls.</p> <p>Test the development role against representative development and production permissions without making a production change. See <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_create_policy-examples.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AWS IAM role policy examples (online)</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"minimize-the-shell-environment\">Minimize the shell environment</h4> <p>In <code>/etc/codex/config.toml</code> for a system-wide starting configuration, or <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> for one user, configure which environment variables spawned commands inherit:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[shell_environment_policy]\ninherit = \"core\"\nignore_default_excludes = false\n\n[shell_environment_policy.filters]\n\"AWS_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"AZURE_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"GITHUB_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_TOKEN\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_SECRET\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_PASSWORD\" = \"exclude\"\n</code></pre> <p>This is a configurable default, not an enforced credential boundary. User settings, shell startup or snapshots, credential helpers, and other credential sources may still expose credentials.</p> <p>In <code>requirements.toml</code>, use the following optional restrictions to disable login shells and shell snapshots if they are compatible with your workflow:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_login_shell = false\n\n[features]\nshell_snapshot = false\n</code></pre> <p>Pilot these settings because some workflows depend on login shell initialization.</p> <p>Start Codex with harmless synthetic variables matching the filters and inspect a spawned command's environment; also verify a login shell request is rejected if disabled. Independently verify that the execution identity cannot access real production credentials. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=shell-environment-policy\">Shell environment policy</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"prompt-for-selected-high-impact-commands\">Prompt for selected high-impact commands</h4> <p>Add requirements rules for the commands your organization uses. For example:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { token = \"push\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review Git pushes.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"terraform\" }, { token = \"apply\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review infrastructure changes.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"kubectl\" }, { any_of = [\"apply\", \"delete\", \"patch\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review cluster changes.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"python3\" }, { token = \"manage.py\" }, { token = \"migrate\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review database migrations.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>Use <code>forbidden</code> for a command that must never run; replace or add patterns for the actual deployment and migration tools.</p> <p>These rules match command prefixes and do not cover every equivalent action. Test absolute paths, wrappers, alternate argument orders, other clients, and other tools separately.</p> <p>On a disposable target, run a normal command, request a listed action, deny the approval request, and verify the action did not execute. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-command-rules-from-requirements\">Enforcing command rules</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"enforce-repository-and-deployment-approvals-outside-codex\">Enforce repository and deployment approvals outside Codex</h4> <p>In your source-control platform, protect production branches such as <code>main</code>: require reviewed changes and the necessary approvals and status checks, and review which roles can bypass those requirements. Configure deployment approval in the relevant deployment system and database privileges in the database itself.</p> <p>Use a disposable repository to verify a reviewed change works and an unauthorized direct push is rejected. These controls apply regardless of whether an action was requested through Codex.</p> <h4 id=\"verify_7\">Verify</h4> <p>On a disposable test target:</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Run a normal workspace task. Expected: The task succeeds.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Attempt a representative sensitive command and deny the approval request. Expected: The denied command does not run.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Verify the Codex identity cannot obtain production write access.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Restart the client and repeat.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"boundaries_7\">Boundaries</h4> <p>Prefix rules are not a complete operating system policy engine. Pair them with sandboxing, least-privilege OS accounts, network restrictions, cloud IAM, branch protection, deployment approvals, and database authorization.</p>  <p><a id=\"reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\">Reduce the risk of publishing internal code</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-reduce-the-risk-of-publishing-internal-code\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p><strong>Uses these configuration recipes:</strong> Protect secrets and unrelated repositories; Restrict network destinations; Allow only approved MCP servers; Require approval for sensitive commands.</p> <p>Developers use Codex with internal repositories, and you want to reduce the risk of code being sent to a public repository, public code snippet, or other unapproved destination.</p> <ul> <li> <p>Add exact deny paths for known credential directories and unrelated repositories, and minimize environment variables passed to spawned commands.</p> </li> <li> <p>When agent internet access is required, use a narrow allowlist of approved domains. Allow only approved MCP server names and identities, and require review for selected publishing commands or deny them, such as <code>git push</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Use source control hooks and permissions, policies for public repositories and code snippets, and data loss prevention controls appropriate to your organization.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"deny-access-to-unrelated-repositories-and-credential-files\">Deny access to unrelated repositories and credential files</h4> <p>In administrator-managed <code>requirements.toml</code>, add exact paths that the local agent must not read:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.filesystem]\ndeny_read = [\"~/.ssh\", \"~/.aws/credentials\", \"~/.kube/config\", \"/work/unrelated-repository\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Replace the examples with paths present on your managed devices. This managed deny applies across allowed permission profiles; a deny configured only inside one custom profile does not protect users who select a different profile. Prefer exact known paths and test any glob patterns at the depths you need.</p> <p>On native Windows, managed <code>deny_read</code> covers direct file tools but does not by itself prevent shell subprocess reads; use OS identities and file access control lists as well.</p> <p>Create harmless canary files in the denied locations. Under every allowed profile, verify that direct and shell reads of those files are denied and that an approved source file can be read. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-deny-read-requirements\">Managed deny-read requirements</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"minimize-credential-bearing-shell-variables\">Minimize credential-bearing shell variables</h4> <p>In <code>/etc/codex/config.toml</code> or <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, set a narrow inherited environment:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[shell_environment_policy]\ninherit = \"core\"\nignore_default_excludes = false\n\n[shell_environment_policy.filters]\n\"AWS_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"AZURE_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"GITHUB_*\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_TOKEN\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_SECRET\" = \"exclude\"\n\"*_PASSWORD\" = \"exclude\"\n</code></pre> <p>This setting reduces inherited variables but is user-configurable and does not prevent access to credential files, helpers, shell startup content, or other identities.</p> <p>Where appropriate, an administrator can also add these restrictions to <code>requirements.toml</code> and pilot them to assess their effect on developer workflows:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_login_shell = false\n\n[features]\nshell_snapshot = false\n</code></pre> <p>Test with synthetic canary variables and a separate least-privilege execution identity. Do not use real credentials. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=shell-environment-policy\">Shell environment policy</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"allow-only-approved-network-destinations-for-local-commands\">Allow only approved network destinations for local commands</h4> <p>If local sandboxed commands need network access, merge both the profile and managed-proxy settings below into <code>requirements.toml</code>, replacing the domains with approved endpoints:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"corp_network\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\ncorp_network = true\n\n[permissions.corp_network]\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.corp_network.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[experimental_network]\nenabled = true\nmanaged_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n[experimental_network.domains]\n\"git.corp.example\" = \"allow\"\n\"registry.corp.example\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>The network-enabled profile grants the sandboxed command a network path, while the managed proxy restricts its destinations. Deploy them together; a network-enabled profile without the intended managed proxy does not apply the same destination restrictions. If internet access is unnecessary, keep network access disabled.</p> <p>Do not combine the canonical <code>domains</code> table with legacy <code>allowed_domains</code> or <code>denied_domains</code> settings; migrate and test the existing policy.</p> <p>Restart the client and verify that an approved endpoint works and an unapproved endpoint is blocked.</p> <p>These settings are experimental; validate OS and client support, and configure private-network access separately if an approved host resolves to a private address. An approved full sandbox escalation can bypass the command proxy.</p> <p>This network control for local commands does not by itself cover web search, apps, MCP servers, browser activity, or cloud environments, which need separate policies. An approved hosting domain may also contain public repositories or public code snippets, so destination allowlisting alone is not content protection. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">Network access requirements</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"configure-cloud-environment-networking-separately\">Configure cloud environment networking separately</h4> <p>In the current Codex Cloud interface, open the environment configuration. Leave <strong>Allow Codex to access internet</strong> off. If you need internet access, turn it on and choose <strong>Custom domains only</strong>, add the exact required hosts under <strong>Additional allowed domains</strong>, then save the configuration, publish or republish the environment, and test a new task.</p> <p>Review service authentication and capabilities independently.</p> <p>Legacy Codex Cloud uses a separate per-environment agent internet setting with controls for allowed domains and HTTP methods; setup scripts have their own access behavior.</p> <p>Test the environment version you actually use. See <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">Connect to services</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">legacy internet settings</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"restrict-mcp-servers-and-prompt-before-git-pushes\">Restrict MCP servers and prompt before Git pushes</h4> <p>Allow only approved local MCP server identities in <code>requirements.toml</code>. For example:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.internal]\nidentity = { url = \"https://mcp.corp.example/mcp\" }\n</code></pre> <p>Configure the actual server separately in the user or managed-default <code>config.toml</code>, using the matching name and URL and the authentication required by your server:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[mcp_servers.internal]\nurl = \"https://mcp.corp.example/mcp\"\n</code></pre> <p>The requirement only permits a matching server and does not create it. Review that server's own access and tools.</p> <p>Verify that the approved server can connect and that a server with a different name or URL is not allowed.</p> <p>Add a managed command rule to request review before a representative publishing command:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { token = \"push\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review the destination before publishing.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>Pair this with <code>allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]</code> and <code>allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]</code> as top-level requirements if a person must review.</p> <p>Deny a test push and confirm it does not run.</p> <p>Prefix matching does not cover every wrapper, API client, app, or alternate publishing path.</p> <h4 id=\"restrict-public-publishing-with-source-control-and-dlp-controls\">Restrict public publishing with source control and DLP controls</h4> <p>In your source control platform, restrict public repository creation and limit who can change repository visibility, where supported. Review bypass permissions for privileged users. Separately check personal accounts, code snippets, external organizations, and token access: organization settings do not govern every destination.</p> <p>In the relevant repository, require reviewed changes and review branch protection and bypass permissions.</p> <p>In your organization's DLP console, configure a pilot policy for sensitive source-code content and the upload or repository channels your DLP product actually covers; set the action to block and test with an inert synthetic marker.</p> <p>Where the source-control platform supports server-side controls, configure and enforce reviewed hooks for repositories receiving pushes.</p> <p>Use authorized test destinations and confirm blocking before any publication.</p> <h4 id=\"verify_8\">Verify</h4> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Create harmless canaries and confirm that attempts to read denied files fail.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] If network access is enabled, check that an approved destination succeeds and an unapproved destination is blocked; otherwise, verify network requests are blocked.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] With a harmless marker and disposable test accounts or destinations, verify representative attempts to publish publicly are blocked before publication.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"boundaries_8\">Boundaries</h4> <p>An allowed domain can still host public content. Configure web search, apps, MCP servers, browser activity, and Codex Cloud separately; local sandbox rules do not control them.</p>  <p><a id=\"keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\">Keep Codex from changing its own safeguards</h3> <p><persistentchecklist id=\"codex-security-cookbook-keep-codex-from-changing-its-own-safeguards\"></persistentchecklist></p> <p><strong>Uses these configuration recipes:</strong> Restrict permission profiles and Full Access; Allow only approved MCP servers; Restrict network destinations; Protect secrets and unrelated repositories; Require approval for sensitive commands.</p> <p>You want the restrictions governing Codex to remain in force when it works in a repository or a user changes local configurations.</p> <ul> <li> <p>Deliver supported permission, approval, MCP, network, and command restrictions as administrator-enforced requirements.</p> </li> <li> <p>Install hook scripts in an administrator-managed directory and use managed-only hooks if user, project, and plugin hooks should be ignored.</p> </li> <li> <p>Protect policy files and the configuration distribution path with operating system and device management controls. Run Codex without the privileges needed to change those protections.</p> </li> <li> <p>Review the effective configuration for each client and environment in scope.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"enforce-the-permitted-profiles-approvals-server-identities-and-commands\">Enforce the permitted profiles, approvals, server identities, and commands</h4> <p>Merge reviewed settings into the administrator-managed <code>requirements.toml</code>, rather than relying on a user's or repository's editable settings. For example:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allowed_approval_policies = [\"on-request\"]\nallowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\"]\ndefault_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\n\":read-only\" = true\n\":workspace\" = true\n\n[mcp_servers.internal]\nidentity = { url = \"https://mcp.corp.example/mcp\" }\n\n[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { token = \"push\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Review Git pushes.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>This example permits two profiles, requires a human reviewer when approval is needed, permits a matching MCP identity, and prompts for a particular command prefix.</p> <p>Configure the MCP server itself separately and add the organization's other sensitive command patterns. Add global file restrictions and network restrictions when needed; requirements constrain supported settings but cannot substitute for the identity, service, and destination controls outside Codex.</p> <p>Managed profile allowlists require Codex 0.138.0 or later.</p> <p>On a pilot client, attempt to select a disallowed profile, change a restricted user or project setting, and use a non-allowlisted MCP identity; verify each is rejected or remains constrained after restart. Deny a test command approval and verify the command does not run.</p> <h4 id=\"add-managed-filesystem-and-network-restrictions\">Add managed filesystem and network restrictions</h4> <p>To deny sensitive local files across allowed profiles, add the following table to <code>requirements.toml</code> and replace the examples with your organization's paths:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.filesystem]\ndeny_read = [\"~/.ssh\", \"~/.aws/credentials\", \"~/.kube/config\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Where sandboxed local commands need network access, define an allowed network-enabled profile and managed destinations together:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"corp_network\"\n\n[allowed_permission_profiles]\ncorp_network = true\n\n[permissions.corp_network]\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.corp_network.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[experimental_network]\nenabled = true\nmanaged_allowed_domains_only = true\n\n[experimental_network.domains]\n\"git.corp.example\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>Choose this profile policy in place of the earlier <code>:workspace</code> default, or merge both profile names deliberately; do not paste duplicate keys or tables. If commands do not need network, keep it disabled.</p> <p>Verify exact denied paths using harmless files, and verify approved and unapproved destinations.</p> <p>On native Windows, also use file access control lists because shell subprocess reads are not covered by managed <code>deny_read</code> alone.</p> <p>Local command networking does not govern other clients, apps, connectors, or cloud execution.</p> <h4 id=\"install-and-enforce-administrator-managed-hooks\">Install and enforce administrator-managed hooks</h4> <p>Have an administrator install a reviewed hook program in a root-managed directory, such as <code>/etc/codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py</code>, and prevent the Codex user from changing the file or its directory. In <code>requirements.toml</code>, enable hooks and ignore user, project, session, and plugin hooks:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">allow_managed_hooks_only = true\n\n[features]\nhooks = true\n\n[hooks]\nmanaged_dir = \"/etc/codex/hooks\"\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\nmatcher = \"^Bash$\"\n\n[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]\ntype = \"command\"\ncommand = \"/usr/bin/python3 /etc/codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\"\ntimeout = 30\nstatusMessage = \"Checking managed Bash command\"\n</code></pre> <p>The requirement registers the hook but does not install it. For a local macOS integration test, save the following as <code>pre_tool_use_policy.py</code> and have an administrator install it with the commands below.</p> <p>It blocks only the synthetic marker; replace it with a reviewed organization-specific policy before relying on it. Confirm that <code>/usr/bin/python3</code> exists on the target and adjust the interpreter path if necessary:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import json\nimport sys\n\nevent = json.load(sys.stdin)\ncommand = event.get(\"tool_input\", {}).get(\"command\", \"\")\nif \"CODEX_HOOK_TEST_DENY\" in command:\n    print(\"Synthetic test command blocked by managed hook.\", file=sys.stderr)\n    sys.exit(2)\n</code></pre> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo install -d -o root -g wheel -m 755 /etc/codex/hooks\nsudo install -o root -g wheel -m 644 ./pre_tool_use_policy.py /etc/codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py\n</code></pre> <p>Restart the local client, then:</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Confirm a benign Bash command runs.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Confirm a command containing <code>CODEX_HOOK_TEST_DENY</code> is blocked.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Verify that the policy and dependencies cannot be changed by the Codex user.</p> </li> </ul> <p><code>allow_managed_hooks_only</code> skips all non-managed user, project, session, and plugin hooks and may affect other workflows.</p> <p>The Bash matcher does not cover every tool or input supplied later to an existing process; errors, timeouts, or malformed hook responses can allow a tool call to proceed.</p> <p>Cloud-orchestrated Work uses different supported hook handlers. Keep independent OS and service protections. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=enforce-managed-hooks-from-requirements\">Managed hooks</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-0aecdb1ef0cec0a4?anchor=pretooluse\">hook behavior</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"protect-the-policy-and-its-distribution\">Protect the policy and its distribution</h4> <p>On macOS, an administrator can deploy a reviewed and merged requirements file in a root-owned parent directory and prevent the Codex user from writing to the file:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo install -d -o root -g wheel -m 755 /etc/codex\nsudo install -o root -g wheel -m 644 ./requirements.reviewed.toml /etc/codex/requirements.toml\nls -ld /etc/codex\nls -l /etc/codex/requirements.toml\n</code></pre> <p>On Linux, use the appropriate administrator group; on Windows, protect the ProgramData directory and file with administrator-managed access control lists. Restrict who can change the MDM or cloud policy, review changes, and preserve a rollback copy.</p> <p>Using a non-admin pilot account, verify that the account cannot modify the policy, parent directory, or managed hook program; try a weaker user or project setting, restart Codex, and verify the enforced behavior persists.</p> <p>Test each client and environment in scope rather than assuming all clients load the same policy.</p> <h4 id=\"verify_9\">Verify</h4> <p>Using a non-admin test identity:</p> <ul> <li> <p>[ ] Attempt to edit the managed policy. Expected: The managed file cannot be changed.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Attempt to weaken a restriction through user or project configuration. Expected: Weaker settings are rejected or remain constrained.</p> </li> <li> <p>[ ] Restart the client and verify the restrictions still apply.</p> </li> </ul> <h4 id=\"boundaries_9\">Boundaries</h4> <p>A user or agent with sufficient host administrator privileges may be able to change the surrounding protections. User-editable settings alone are not a substitute for administrator-managed requirements.</p>  <h2 id=\"source-pages\">Source pages</h2> <ul> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85\">Advanced Configuration</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Authentication</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-668fd22585595004\">Sandboxing</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">Auto-review</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">Agent approvals and security</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">Agent internet access</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-73011d6801bc30d3\">Cloud environments</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-b74b7c76c711106d\">Recommended configuration</a></p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-9d54fab7ad7294d0\">Compliance API and audit events</a></p> </li> </ul>"};
